{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat AGENTS.md README.md apps/docs/pages/getting-started.md apps/docs/pages/stability.md apps/docs/pages/conformance.md'","aggregated_output":"# Working in this repository\n\nAxylo is a browser charting library. The product is specified in `docs/02-product-spec.md`; the build plan is `docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md`. Read the sections your task names before writing code.\n\n## Rules for every change\n\n- **Contracts are fixed.** `packages/axylo/src/core/contracts.ts` and `packages/axylo/src/public.ts` are the interfaces between modules. Do not edit them. If your work needs a change there, stop that part, finish what you can, and describe the exact change you need in your report.\n- **Stay in your files.** Each task lists the directories it owns. Do not create, edit or delete anything else, including configuration and other modules. Several agents work in this checkout at the same time.\n- **No runtime dependencies.** Use the platform (`Intl`, typed arrays). Dev dependencies already installed: TypeScript, Vitest, fast-check, esbuild, Playwright (system Chrome), axe-core. The network is not available; do not try to install anything.\n- **No DOM at import time.** A module that needs the DOM takes it as a parameter or touches it inside a function. Pure stages (data, scales, formatting, layout, scene) must run in Node.\n- **Hot paths use typed arrays.** No object per data point, no array of arrays per data point, no copying of large columns that the contracts say are borrowed.\n- **Never guess silently.** Anything that cannot be drawn as given is reported through the `DiagnosticSink` with the code from spec section 3.5.\n- **Tests come from the spec.** Expected values are derived from the specification and the fixture data, never by running the implementation and pasting its output. Use fast-check property tests for invariants (\"for any data …\"). A test you cannot make pass is reported as failing; do not weaken it or change its expected value.\n- **TypeScript strict, no `any`** in exported signatures. `__DEV__` is a build-time boolean (true in tests).\n- **Comments say why**, not what. Names are plain words.\n- **Do not commit.** The coordinator commits.\n\n## Commands\n\n```bash\nnpm run typecheck      # tsc over the library, its tests, and the API examples in docs/api\nnpm test               # unit and property tests (Node)\nnpm run build          # packages/axylo/dist\nnpm run test:browser   # browser tests against the built bundle, in system Chrome\n```\n\n## Your report\n\nEnd with a short report: what you built (files), what you verified and how (paste the last lines of the test run), what is not done or not verified, any contract change you need, and any place where the spec was ambiguous and what you assumed. Keep \"ran and saw\" apart from \"expect\".\n# Axylo\n\nEveryday charts for web apps and dashboards: faithful to the data, readable everywhere, accessible by default, and honest when the data is wrong.\n\nStatus: design, revision 2 (2026-11-18). No library code exists yet. Revision 1 was reviewed by three independent reviewers; revision 2 answers them and has not itself been reviewed.\n\n| Document | Contents |\n|---|---|\n| [docs/01-landscape.md](docs/01-landscape.md) | Phase 1a. Adoption evidence for browser charting libraries and a same-task comparison of ten of them. |\n| [docs/02-product-spec.md](docs/02-product-spec.md) | Phase 1b. What Axylo is, first-release scope, acceptance criteria, and the decisions left to the owner. |\n| [docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md](docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md) | Phase 2. Public API, architecture and milestones for a 1.0 written in TypeScript. |\n| [docs/04-plan-performance.md](docs/04-plan-performance.md) | Phase 3. How performance is measured, improved and guarded, and what would justify Rust. |\n| [docs/api/](docs/api/examples.ts) | API examples, type-checked against the real package (`packages/axylo/src/public.ts`) by `npm run typecheck`. |\n| [research/](research/README.md) | The data, harness and screenshots behind Phase 1. |\n| [reviews/2026-11-18/ledger.md](reviews/2026-11-18/ledger.md) | The three reviews, what each finding led to, and what was rejected or left undone. |\n# Getting started\n\nAfter this page you can install Axylo, draw a first chart from rows with npm or a script tag, use it inside a framework, get the five things right that most often make a daily chart wrong, and know what to check when a chart looks wrong.\n\n## Install\n\nWith npm and a bundler:\n\n```sh\nnpm install axylo\n```\n\nWith a script tag, load one file from the package's `dist` folder: `axylo.iife.js` is the development build (it prints hints), `axylo.iife.min.js` the production build. Both define one global, `Axylo`.\n\nThe package has no runtime dependencies. Its module build comes in two flavours, chosen by the `development` export condition: a bundler that resolves that condition gets the development build, which also prints hints; otherwise you get the production build, which prints errors and warnings only. The styles need no import: the library adopts its stylesheet as a constructed style sheet, or, where that is not possible, adds a `<style>` element. A page that prefers to load the stylesheet itself can link `axylo/axylo.css`, and the library then uses that copy. The packed packages are tested in Chrome under a Content Security Policy without `unsafe-inline`, with the stylesheet adopted and with it linked (`tests/dist/csp.browser.test.ts`).\n\nTypeScript 5.4 or later gives you checked field names. The package's declarations refer to DOM types (`HTMLElement`), so a TypeScript project needs the `DOM` library in `lib`, also for code that only calls `validate()` in Node. Plain JavaScript works the same without types.\n\n## The first chart\n\nEach of these runs live below its source. They are kept in the repository as the files the package's distribution tests install and render (`examples/first-chart`), and each says what page it needs around it.\n\nWith npm and a bundler:\n\n[example: first-chart-npm](../../../examples/first-chart/npm.ts)\n\nWith a script tag:\n\n[example: first-chart-script](../../../examples/first-chart/script.html)\n\nPoint at a chart, or press Tab to reach its plot and use the arrow keys, or open its table. The only required options are `data` and the fields: `x` and `y` (and `series` for long rows), or `label` and `value` for a donut. `title` is not required, but without it (or `labelledBy`) the chart gets a generated name and warning [AX140](diagnostics.md#ax140).\n\n## Using Axylo in a framework\n\nNo framework adapter ships in 1.0; adapters for React, Vue, Svelte and others are planned after 1.0. Axylo is called from any framework's own hooks: create the chart when its element exists, call `update()` when your data or options change, and call `destroy()` when the element goes away. Creating, destroying and creating again on the same element is supported; it is the sequence a framework's development mode produces.\n\nThe lifecycle as three plain functions:\n\n[example: lifecycle](../examples/lifecycle.ts)\n\n## Daily data: five things to get right\n\nThe first chart above has months as calendar dates and numbers as numbers. Data from an API is often less tidy. Here is a daily metric as many back ends send it, and the chart that reads it correctly:\n\n[example: daily-sessions](../examples/daily-sessions.ts)\n\n1. **Days sent as UTC midnights.** For daily totals sent as `'2026-02-24T00:00:00Z'` (or as epoch milliseconds of a UTC midnight), use `x: { field: 'day', type: 'date' }`. Without it they are instants, shown in the viewer's time zone, so west of UTC each value appears on the day before, with only hint [AX111](diagnostics.md#ax111). Strings such as `'2026-02-24'` need no `type`. See [the three kinds of time](data.md#three-kinds-of-time).\n2. **Numbers sent as strings.** For decimal strings, use `y: { field: 'sessions', parse: 'number' }`. They are converted to JavaScript numbers, which are exact for integers up to 9,007,199,254,740,991; a larger count is rounded without a warning, so check exact counts with `Number.isSafeInteger` first. A field that mixes numbers and strings is not converted at all. See [numbers sent as strings](data.md#numbers-sent-as-strings).\n3. **Row order.** Pass rows in any order. Axylo sorts dated rows by x and keeps each value with its own row; categories keep the order in which they first appear.\n4. **A day with no data.** When you know a day is missing, send it, with `sessions: null`. Axylo can infer a missing day only between two rows of a series that has at least three rows at a regular step, and never before the first row or after the last; with two rows, or an irregular series, the line runs straight across the missing day. See [missing values](data.md#missing-values).\n5. **A misspelled field.** On error [AX101](diagnostics.md#ax101), compare the field you named with the keys the message lists, which come from your rows, and use the suggested spelling when one is offered. With no rows at all the keys cannot be checked, so the error appears once data arrives. With typed rows in TypeScript the typo does not compile.\n\n## What you get without asking\n\n| Feature | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| Name and summary | `title` is the visible heading and the chart's accessible name. In a card that already has a heading, `labelledBy` names the chart with it. The chart's description is a generated summary: chart type, series, the x range with its time zone, the value range, each series' minimum, maximum and last value, and anything left out. `description` adds to it. |\n| Table view | \"View as table\" shows the data as an HTML table, 100 rows per page; above 100 rows a pager and a \"Jump to\" field appear. |\n| Keyboard | Up to two tab stops while the table is closed: the plot, where the arrow keys move through the points and its value text describes the point in focus, and the toolbar, when the chart has controls. A compact chart has none. See [the keyboard map](accessibility.md#the-keyboard-map). |\n| Reading values | Pointing in the plot shows what is there, by the chart type's rule (nearest x for line and area, the band for bars, the nearest point within 24 px for scatter, the slice for a donut). The tooltip sits against the marks it describes, not at the pointer, and lets the pointer through; rest on a point to move onto its tooltip, click to pin it. In a small plot the same text goes in a strip under the plot. Escape closes it. See [reading values with a pointer](accessibility.md#reading-values-with-a-pointer). |\n| Colour and more than colour | Eight default colours that work in light and dark pages. Lines and areas also carry marker shapes, repeated in the legend and tooltip; bars take fill patterns past the eighth series. |\n| Page theme | Font, text colour and light or dark come from the page. The chart repaints when the page switches scheme. |\n| Size | The chart fills its container's width. With no height set, the plot is 16:9 of the width, between 160 and 480 px tall. It follows the container as it resizes, with no call from you. |\n| States | No rows shows \"No data to show.\" inside the plot, never an empty frame or an invented axis. `state` sets loading, empty and error messages of your own. |\n| Diagnostics | Anything that cannot be drawn as given is reported with a code, the field and rows where they apply, and the fix. When values were left out or changed, the reader is told too. |\n\nThe four states, each a chart with no rows:\n\n[example: states](../examples/states.ts)\n\n## When it looks wrong: three things to check\n\n**1. Read the diagnostics.** Open the browser console. Every problem Axylo found is printed once per code per page as `[axylo AX…] \"Chart title\": what is wrong, where, and how to fix it`. The same list is on the chart as `chart.diagnostics`, current as soon as the call returns. Each code has a page under [Diagnostics](diagnostics.md). Hints, which are advice rather than problems, print in the development build only.\n\n**2. Check which kind of x Axylo read.** A date off by one day almost always means instants where calendar dates were meant (item 1 above). If the tooltip heading names a time zone, Axylo read instants. If the x labels are your strings in input order, it read categories.\n\n**3. Check the values without drawing.** `validate()` runs the chart's input checks in Node or in a test, with the same options as the chart. It returns every finding from those checks, including hints, in every build. In a project with `axylo` installed, save this plain JavaScript as `check.mjs` and run `node check.mjs`.\n\n[source: examples/node/check.mjs](../examples/node/check.mjs)\n\nTo check the monthly visitors chart above in TypeScript, pass its rows and options to `validate()`.\n\n```ts\nimport { validate } from 'axylo';\n\nconst problems = validate('line', { title: 'Monthly visitors', data: rows, x: 'month', y: 'visitors' });\nfor (const d of problems) console.log(d.code, d.level, d.message);\n```\n\nNumbers that arrive as strings, a missing day, two rows for the same day and a misspelled field each have their own code; [Your data](data.md) covers each case.\n\n## Next\n\nRead [Your data](data.md) next: it explains the three shapes rows can take, how dates are read, and what happens to missing values.\n# Stability and releases\n\nAfter this page you know what Axylo promises between releases, which browsers and tools it supports, under what licence it ships, and what is not in 1.0.\n\n## Status\n\nThis documentation describes Axylo 1.0, which has not been released. The library in this repository is a pre-release (package version 0.0.0) and has not been published to npm. Until 1.0 is tagged, anything may change.\n\n## Versions\n\nAxylo follows semantic versioning:\n\n| Release | May contain |\n|---|---|\n| Patch (1.0.1) | Fixes that do not change documented behaviour, except to make it match the documentation. |\n| Minor (1.1.0) | New options, chart features and diagnostics, and deprecations. Nothing documented stops working. |\n| Major (2.0.0) | Removals and other breaking changes, at most one major version a year. |\n\n**Deprecation.** A deprecated API keeps working for at least one minor release and at least six months, and its documentation says what to use instead. The documented examples of the previous release must still compile and run against each release candidate.\n\n**What the promise covers.** The functions and types exported by `axylo` (declared in `packages/axylo/src/public.ts`), the diagnostic codes and their levels, the CSS custom properties documented on [Theming](theming.md), and the keys of `messages`. Match diagnostics on `code`; their messages are written for people.\n\n**What it does not cover.** The chart's internal DOM structure and class names, the custom properties listed as internal, the roles the plot uses to talk to assistive technology (chosen without screen-reader rehearsal, and changeable in a minor release if rehearsal shows a better pattern), and the exact pixels of the drawing.\n\n## Supported environments\n\nThese are the targets of 1.0 (spec section 2.3). What has been verified so far is listed after the table.\n\n| Dimension | 1.0 target |\n|---|---|\n| Browsers | The current and previous major versions of Chrome, Edge and Firefox, and of Safari on macOS and iOS. |\n| TypeScript | 5.4 and later. Plain JavaScript is fully supported; types are an aid, not a requirement. |\n| Frameworks | None needed. No adapter ships in 1.0; the library is called from any framework's mount and unmount hooks. |\n| Node | Importing `axylo` touches no DOM. `validate()` returns every finding from the input checks, including hints, in every build. For a check you can run with `node check.mjs`, see [Diagnostics](diagnostics.md#validate-in-node-and-in-tests). Charts themselves need a browser. |\n| Text direction | Left-to-right pages. Right-to-left text renders, but the layout is not mirrored. |\n| Transformed ancestors | A chart inside a translated or scaled element (any positive scale, uniform or not) is exact. Inside a rotated, skewed or mirrored element it draws, but pointing and tooltip placement are wrong: not supported in 1.0. |\n| Built-in text | English. Reader-facing messages are replaceable through `messages`; diagnostics keep their English wording. |\n\nTested so far, on Linux: the library's acceptance tests in Chrome and in Playwright's builds of Firefox and WebKit (Playwright's WebKit, not Safari), and the distribution tests in Chrome (they install the packed package, also under a Content Security Policy). Safari itself, Edge, Windows, macOS and phones have not been tested. No screen-reader rehearsal was done for 1.0. What the acceptance checker recorded for this revision is on the [conformance page](conformance.md); see also [Accessibility](accessibility.md#what-has-and-has-not-been-verified).\n\n## Known limits\n\nThese are known in 1.0 and not fixed in it. Each says what you would see.\n\n- **Closed shadow roots.** In a chart inside a closed shadow root, Escape can be taken by another chart on the page instead of this one. When the chart sits behind a slot inside a closed root, the wrapper around that slot can cut off its tooltip.\n- **A dense ring of scatter points.** Thousands of points packed in a ring just outside the distance at which points count as being at the same place (half a pixel) can make hovering over that spot slow.\n- **A formatter that fails only sometimes.** A formatter that throws for a value on one call and returns text on another can leave [AX160](diagnostics.md#ax160) reported beside a label that reads normally.\n- **CSS aimed at the plot's own column.** The readout strip is measured outside the plot's column, so CSS that applies only inside that column (a container query on it, for example) is not taken into account. A small plot can then show no readout although the strip would fit.\n- **A hold that starts early.** When only the tooltip's size or position changes while the pointer rests on a datum, the rest already spent counts towards the new one, so the hold can begin before a quarter of a second has passed.\n- **One language for the focused point.** The plot's value text is marked with one language, even when the messages it is built from are not all in the same language (some translated, others built in).\n- **Patterns in compact bar charts.** Neighbouring series always differ by fill pattern, but series further apart can share one, since there are four, and then differ by colour only.\n- **Very small numbers.** On an axis of values around 1e-43 or smaller, ticks can be missing or not match their labels.\n- **The end labels of a scatter chart's x axis.** That axis ends on a drawn tick at each end (spec F7) and reserves no extra room for the labels there, so long tick labels are wrapped or rotated to fit rather than left out. On a narrow plot the axis can take a coarser, wider range so that its ends are ticks.\n\n## Agent rehearsal\n\nOn release candidate `1e93128`, Codex CLI `gpt-6.2-sol` and Claude Code `claude-opus-5-5` each passed three uncoached attempts on each of four fixed line and bar tasks. All 24 attempts passed after the transcript re-audit. In Chrome 158, each saved answer passed the reader checks without error diagnostics on both inputs in the Los Angeles and Tokyo time zones. This agent rehearsal gives no estimate of general success and provides no evidence of human or screen-reader usability or WCAG conformance. The prompt restricted agents to the published docs for guidance, and the audit checked recorded access. The filesystem did not enforce docs-only access.\n\n## Size and memory\n\nThe size targets of 1.0, set by the owner after measuring the built library: 116 KiB gzipped for a page that uses everything, and 98 KiB for a page that uses only `line`, the stylesheet included. They are not a release gate yet. The last measurement (`npm run size`, which measures the packed files the way a bundler would):\n\n[table: sizes](../../../bench/size-latest.json)\n\nMemory: once a chart opened on typed columns has been appended to, Axylo keeps its own exact copy of x and y and one state byte per point, 17 bytes per point against the 16 of your two `Float64Array` columns. The memory budget of 1.0 is therefore 1.1 times the size of your two columns, for 1,000,000 points, with no upward trend over 1,000 cycles of create and destroy. This documentation shows no memory measurement. A chart that is never appended to reads typed columns in place where it can (see [what Axylo reads, keeps and copies](data.md#what-axylo-reads-keeps-and-copies)).\n\n## Licence\n\nMIT, for everything: zoom, accessibility and every chart type included. There is no commercial edition and no feature behind a licence key. The library has no runtime dependencies; third-party notices, if any, are checked at each release.\n\n## What is not in 1.0\n\nNot planned for 1.0, with the reason it can wait:\n\n| Not in 1.0 | Why it can wait |\n|---|---|\n| More chart types (histogram first, then heatmap, box plot, candlestick, radar, treemap, sankey, gauge, maps) | Each must pass the same acceptance criteria as the five types in 1.0. |\n| Log and other non-linear scales | They need their own rules for zero and negative values. |\n| A second y axis | Often misleading; it needs a design that keeps the zero baseline honest. |\n| Annotations beyond reference lines and bands | Placing free text interacts with label fitting. |\n| Linked charts (shared cursor, brush) | Needs a small coordination API. |\n| Export to PNG, SVG or CSV, and print styling | CSV from the table view comes first. |\n| Server-side rendering to SVG | The drawing model makes it possible later. |\n| Right-to-left layout mirroring | Stated as a limit in 1.0. |\n| Quarters and ISO weeks as calendar periods | Treat them as ordered categories in 1.0. |\n| Nested field paths (`'user.country'`) | Flatten rows before passing them. |\n| Framework adapters (React, Vue, Svelte, Angular) and a custom element | Thin wrappers over the same handle; until then a chart is created and destroyed from the framework's own hooks (see [Getting started](getting-started.md#using-axylo-in-a-framework)). |\n| A plugin or custom-mark API | Waits until two real uses show its shape. |\n| Translations of built-in text | `messages` makes reader-facing ones possible; only English ships. |\n| Workers, WebGL or WebGPU | Decided from measurements. |\n\nNot the audience for 1.0: analysts exploring data in notebooks, designers building bespoke graphics, and teams that need maps, financial charts or network diagrams.\n# Conformance table\n\nAfter this page you know which WCAG 2.2 success criteria the specification cites, what Axylo does for each, what evidence was recorded for them when this site was built, why no test covers the other level A and AA criteria, and what that evidence cannot show.\n\nThis page is generated each time the site is built, from the specification's criterion rows and from the report of the acceptance checker. It says only what they record; where they record nothing, it says \"not recorded\". It is not a claim that a page using Axylo conforms: conformance belongs to a whole page, and the page is yours.\n\n## What the evidence can and cannot show\n\nOn release candidate `1e93128`, Codex CLI `gpt-6.2-sol` and Claude Code `claude-opus-5-5` each passed three uncoached attempts on each of four fixed line and bar tasks. All 24 attempts passed after the transcript re-audit. In Chrome 158, each saved answer passed the reader checks without error diagnostics on both inputs in the Los Angeles and Tokyo time zones. This agent rehearsal gives no estimate of general success and provides no evidence of human or screen-reader usability or WCAG conformance. The prompt restricted agents to the published docs for guidance, and the audit checked recorded access. The filesystem did not enforce docs-only access.\n\n- **Automated checks only.** The acceptance tests check structure: names, roles, focus order, contrast, sizes and text. They do not show that a person using a screen reader understands a chart.\n- **No screen-reader rehearsal was done for 1.0.** No screen reader has been run against these charts, so nothing in this documentation says what a screen reader speaks.\n- **No usability testing with disabled people.** Axylo is built by one person with no access to study participants, and no claim of usability testing with disabled users is made.\n- **Engines, not browsers.** Browser results are recorded per engine: Chrome, and Firefox and WebKit as built by Playwright. WebKit results are Playwright's WebKit, not Safari. Nothing has been run on a phone.\n- **Right-to-left pages.** Right-to-left text renders, but the chart's layout is not mirrored.\n- **Transformed ancestors.** A chart inside a translated or scaled element is exact. Inside a rotated, skewed or mirrored element it draws, but pointing and tooltip placement are wrong; that is not supported in 1.0.\n\n## What you must supply\n\nAxylo can only describe what it is given. A chart needs a meaningful name (`title`, or `labelledBy` pointing at your heading), series names, units on value fields, and the page's own structure around it. A compact chart fed by `append` has no Pause control, since it has no toolbar: a page that streams into compact tiles must give the reader its own way to pause them (WCAG 2.2.2). [Accessibility](accessibility.md#what-you-must-supply) has the details.\n\n## The criteria the specification cites\n\n[table: conformance](../../../acceptance/check.mjs)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
