{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n '\"'^#|[Dd]efer|[Pp]ostpone|[Ll]ater|[Ss]cope|out of|[Zz]oom|[Pp]ause|[Cc]ompact'\"' docs/02-product-spec.md\"","aggregated_output":"1:# Phase 1b: Axylo product specification\n3:Revision 3, 2026-11-18. Revised after three independent reviews ([ledger](../reviews/2026-11-18/ledger.md)), then updated with the owner's decisions of the same day: scatter is in 1.0, a compact variant is in 1.0, and the mark renderer is decided by measurement in M1.\n7:## 1. What Axylo is\n11:It is a framework-neutral TypeScript library under the MIT license. 1.0 ships no framework adapter (owner's decision, 2026-11-18): adapters for React, Vue, Svelte and others come later; in a framework, create the chart when its element exists and call `destroy()` when it goes away.\n15:### 1.1 Why someone would choose it\n29:### 1.2 Who it is for\n35:### 1.3 Principles\n46:## 2. Scope of the first release\n48:### 2.1 Chart types\n57:| Compact variant | Of line, area and bar: marks only, for KPI tiles and sparklines | Which way is it going? | 1.0 (owner's decision) |\n61:The Phase 1 harness exercised line, bar and donut in all ten libraries (donut in nine; uPlot has none). Area and scatter are added on judgment; Phase 1 found no usage data by chart type. Two reviewers argued for fewer types and one for a compact variant; the owner decided on all five types plus the compact variant. Scatter alone needs two-dimensional hit testing and its own navigation model, so it is built last and carries the most schedule risk.\n65:### 2.2 Workflows\n72:| W4 | Watch a live metric | Append without redraw cost growing; a pause control; no announcement flood. |\n73:| W5 | Explore a range | Tooltip anywhere, legend toggle, zoom and pan by single click, drag, touch and keyboard. |\n74:| W6 | Read it another way | Keyboard, screen reader, table view, high contrast, reduced motion, 400% zoom, color-vision deficiency. |\n76:### 2.3 Supported environments\n81:| TypeScript | 5.4 and later. Plain JavaScript is fully supported; types are an aid, not a requirement. |\n87:## 3. Acceptance criteria: the reader's experience\n91:### 3.1 Faithful representation\n99:| F5 | No smoothing. When a line is denser than the screen, the drawing is simplified with this guarantee: for every device-pixel column, and every contiguous run of valid samples inside it, the drawn stroke covers exactly that run's minimum and maximum. A break between two runs is always kept, even when both fall in one pixel column; a slot a series merely has no row for (weekly data beside daily) does not break its line, except inside a stack (F10). Stacked areas are simplified on their cumulative sums. Exact values always come from the data, not the drawing. | Assertions on which source samples the drawing kept, for: one outlier in 1,000,000; two peaks and a gap in one column; alternating valid and missing samples; tied extremes; device pixel ratio 1 and 2; several zoom levels. A sample that the drawing dropped is still reachable by keyboard and in the table | D8, D9 |\n100:| F6 | Numbers and dates are formatted for the page locale. Ticks and readouts are formatted separately: ticks may be compact (`50K`); tooltip, table and announcements show the full integer part, and the fraction with up to six significant digits not counting the fraction's leading zeros (`123,456.75`; `0.000123456`; `0.1 + 0.2` reads `0.3`), with the unit. A custom formatter that throws falls back to the default and raises a diagnostic. | Expected strings for chosen values (large integers, small decimals, percentages, zoned times) in `en-US`, `de-DE` and `en-IN` | R4 |\n101:| F7 | Domains follow one order of precedence: the zoomed view is inside the author's `domain` if given, otherwise inside the automatic domain. This rule is for value axes: the y axis of line, area and bar, and both axes of scatter. A continuous x axis of line, area or bar spans exactly its data and any x reference the chart draws (plus half a band for bars), so a time series is not padded to the next tick; so does a date or time x axis of a scatter, whose numeric x alone follows the tick rule below. After a zoom on x, a y axis with no author domain is fitted again to the values in view, by the same rules. The automatic domain contains all data, the reference lines and bands the chart draws (a compact chart draws none, so `reference` has no effect there and is reported as an option that does not apply) and stack totals, and ends at the first tick at or beyond each extreme (a compact chart draws no ticks, so its domain is the same one without being shown to end on a tick). A field declared `scale: 'percent'` (0–100) or `'ratio'` (0–1) ends at full scale only when its maximum is at least half of full scale and not above it. Nothing is inferred from a label. | Property tests; `bars` ends at 100 when declared `percent`; a 4% maximum ends at the first tick at or above 4% | R1 |\n103:| F9 | Up to eight series, no two share a color. Beyond eight, each series still has a unique combination of color and dash or marker (for bars, full and compact alike: color and fill pattern; for every chart type at least 32 distinct combinations, after which combinations repeat), and AX130 is raised. | Tests with 6, 8 and 12 series | R7 |\n108:### 3.2 Legibility\n112:| L1 | No visible text overlaps other text or is clipped, and no label disappears without the reader being told. Remedies, in order: thin (time, date, numeric and `ordered` category axes only); wrap to at most three lines; rotate up to 45°; truncate in the middle, keeping every label distinct, with the full text in tooltip and table. If labels still do not fit, a visible note (\"Some labels hidden. See table.\") appears and AX143 suggests horizontal bars. Axylo never changes a chart's orientation on its own. | In a browser, as painted text and not in a model (in the engines of section 2.3 where results are recorded), after fonts load: for widths 240–1,920 px and generated label sets, zero intersecting text boxes (1 px tolerance), zero text outside the figure, truncated labels pairwise distinct. Repeated with the WCAG 1.4.12 text-spacing overrides, 200% text-only zoom, and a font that loads late | R2, WCAG 1.4.12, 1.4.4 |\n121:### 3.3 Interaction\n127:| I3 | Zoom on time, date and numeric x axes is reachable four ways: visible Zoom in, Zoom out and Reset buttons that work with single clicks; drag to select a range; `+`, `-`, `0` and Shift+arrows; and pinch when `zoom: 'touch'` is set. The wheel zooms only while the plot has focus, and never when Ctrl or Meta is held, so browser zoom and page scrolling are not intercepted. | Reach a given range by clicks only; by keyboard only; by drag; page scrolls normally with the pointer over an unfocused chart | D7, R12, WCAG 2.5.1, 2.1.1 |\n128:| I4 | Every interaction works with a single pointer without dragging, and by touch. Every control is at least 24×24 CSS px. A one-finger drag on a chart scrolls the page (on a compact chart too: it takes no pointer input at all, C1). | Touch emulation; size checks | WCAG 2.5.7, 2.5.8, 2.5.1, 2.5.3, 4.1.2, 2.1.1 |\n130:| I6 | A chart fed by `append` shows a Pause/Resume control. While paused, new rows are buffered and the view does not move. | Stream test | WCAG 2.2.2 |\n132:### 3.4 Accessibility\n139:| A2 | Keyboard: at most two tab stops while the table is closed, the plot and one toolbar with roving focus that holds legend toggles, zoom, pause and the table control. Every point is reachable in order; the series can be switched; the ends can be reached directly; focus can leave the chart. The focus indicator is at least 2 px and 3:1 and is not hidden by the tooltip. The exact keys and roles are fixed after the pattern spike in M1 (section 8). | Forward and reverse traversal in collapsed, zoomed, streaming and table-open states; focus after hiding the focused series; indicator contrast | R12, WCAG 2.1.1, 2.1.2, 2.4.3, 2.4.7, 2.4.11, 1.4.11 |\n142:| A5 | Series are never distinguished by color alone, in any mode. Each line and area carries a shape marker at its last point and at sparse intervals, repeated in the legend and tooltip; under `prefers-contrast: more` or forced colors, lines also differ by dash. Adjacent filled marks (slices, stacked segments) are separated by at least 1 px of background. Bars are told apart by position and order, and by the legend and tooltip that name them; a compact chart has neither, so there neighbouring bar series also differ by fill pattern (series further apart may share a pattern: four patterns exist). The default palette has eight colors, each at least 3:1 on the default light and dark backgrounds, with a minimum pairwise OKLab difference of 0.10 (provisional) under normal vision and simulated protanopia, deuteranopia and tritanopia. On any other background, Axylo measures the palette against the resolved background and raises AX141 below 3:1. | Palette script scoring the rendered marks of an eight-series chart in both schemes, repeats included; rendered-pixel checks of separators; a grayscale screenshot in which three crossing lines can be told apart by marker | R7, WCAG 1.4.1, 1.4.11 |\n143:| A6 | Content reflows at 320 CSS px and 400% zoom without two-dimensional scrolling. User text-spacing overrides do not clip text. | Viewport and style-override tests in three engines | WCAG 1.4.10, 1.4.12 |\n149:### 3.5 Diagnostic registry\n151:Level decides delivery. **Error**: the chart or a series cannot be drawn; the chart shows its error state. **Warning**: something is not as the author gave it; where that means values were left out or changed (F12), the reader sees the note, and the registry's rows say which codes those are (a warning about an option, a name, a colour or a tick label shows none). **Hint**: advice for the author. Errors and warnings print once per code per page in every build; hints print in development builds only. `strict: true` throws on errors and warnings found while validating a create, `update` or `append` call, synchronously from that call. A diagnostic that can only arise later (a contrast check after the page's CSS changes, a custom formatter that throws when a table page is built, a label that stops fitting after a resize) is reported when it is found and is never thrown. Every diagnostic names the chart; those about data name the field and the first row indexes in the caller's own data.\n176:| AX151 | Warning | `append` rows out of order | Accepted through the slower full update |\n177:| AX131 | Hint | A compact chart has more than one series | Drawn, with dash differences |\n189:### 3.6 Type matrix\n193:| Picking by pointer | Nearest x | Nearest x | The band under the pointer | The slice under the pointer | Nearest point within 24 px; ties go to the later row |\n196:| Zoom | On time, date and numeric x only | On time, date and numeric x only | On time, date and numeric x only | No | Yes, both axes later; x only in 1.0 |\n201:### 3.7 Compact variant\n203:`compact: true` on line, area and bar draws the marks and nothing else: no axes, gridlines, legend, toolbar, table control, zoom or tooltip. It is for a small tile where the page already shows the number and the label.\n207:| C1 | A compact chart is a static image with a full text alternative: `role=\"img\"`, an accessible name (as in A1, including AX140 when none is given) and the generated summary as its description (series, x range, minimum, maximum, last value, omissions). It has no tab stop and no pointer-only behavior, so nothing is available to a mouse that is not available to a keyboard. | Accessibility-tree snapshot; a page of 40 has zero added tab stops, WCAG 1.1.1 |\n210:| C4 | With more than one series, lines differ by dash as well as color, and bars by fill pattern between neighbours (A5), since there is no legend; AX131 suggests one series per compact chart. | Two-series fixture, WCAG 1.4.1 |\n211:| C5 | When values were left out (F12), the description says so and a small marker with a text equivalent appears in the corner. | `dirty` fixture in compact mode, WCAG 1.1.1 |\n213:A compact chart does not give access to every value; the summary is its alternative. An author who needs every value reachable uses the full chart.\n215:### 3.8 What the evidence will and will not show\n219:## 4. Acceptance criteria: the developer's experience\n227:| DX5 | Three methods change a chart: `update` changes options, `append` adds rows, `zoom` sets the view. They apply in call order. Validation and diagnostics are synchronous; drawing is deferred to the next frame; marks, labels, hit testing and the accessibility layer always switch to a new state together. `destroy()` releases everything and cancels pending work. No `resize()` exists. | Sequence tests: replace then append then zoom in one turn; a rejected `strict` update leaves the last valid chart; destroy before the frame; 1,000 create–destroy cycles show no heap growth trend (P7) | D3, R16 |\n233:| DX11 | MIT license for everything, including zoom and accessibility. Third-party notices are verified at release. | Repository; release check | D7 |\n236:## 5. Performance budgets\n244:| P3 | 600-point window, one appended point per display frame at 60 Hz, driven by an independent clock | Main-thread task time per update (per run: task time in the window divided by the number of updates); and frames later than 25 ms | Median over runs; count per 300 frames | 2 ms; 0 | See 01, section 5.3 |\n248:| P8 | 40 compact charts created on one page, each 160 × 48 px with one series | Total main-thread task time | Median of 20 | 80 ms | Not measured in Phase 1 |\n257:## 6. Decisions and the findings behind them\n270:| Three methods with a commit contract | D3. | Changed from \"one update method\": `append` and `zoom` were always there, and their ordering is now specified. |\n276:| Chart types | Judgment, then the owner's decision. | All five in 1.0, plus the compact variant (3.7). |\n277:| MIT, including zoom and accessibility | D7. | Kept. |\n282:## 7. Changes this revision rejected\n291:## 8. Decisions still open\n293:Settled by the owner on 2026-11-18: scatter is in 1.0; the compact variant is in 1.0; the mark renderer stays open until M1 measures both.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
