{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '65,90p' STATUS.md && cat acceptance/README.md\"","aggregated_output":"| `dist-browser` | 26 | 0 | 0 |\n| `browser`, Chrome 158.0.8127.36 | 1175 | 0 | 1 |\n| `browser`, Playwright Firefox 159.0 | 982 | 0 | 194 |\n| `browser`, Playwright WebKit 27.2 (not Safari) | 982 | 0 | 194 |\n\nAny commit after 1e38333, this one included, changes the tree, so these recordings answer for 1e38333 only and must be repeated at the release commit.\n\nRelease check at 1e38333 (`node acceptance/check.mjs --release --results` with the six files; output kept in `acceptance/results/release-check-1e38333.txt`): exit 1, as expected while weak cells exist. Cells: pass 94, fail 0, weak 164, not run 50, not recorded 0, cannot be verified here 11 (5 are A3, waived by the owner), not applicable 257. The matrix (208c56b) would reach 112 passes; the other 18 are a checker defect, below.\n\nTwo defects found at this step, not fixed (the owner said stop):\n1. **The checker's title scanner nests a top-level describe under the one before it.** In `packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/i1-pointer.browser.test.ts` it gives \"I2 legend toggles\" the ancestors `[\"I1 tooltip under the pointer\", \"I2 legend toggles\"]`, so every citation of a test after that point in the file (I1's new below-minimum test, I2, I5, I6: 18 cells) reads \"not run\" with no reason although `browser.json` passes them. Probably brace or template-literal tracking in `acceptance/titles.mjs` since the strengthening pass added code to that file. For the checker's author (a61d95e46b3f0f3aa): a failing scanner test from that file first; also make a \"not run\" with every report passing carry a reason.\n2. **The docs conformance page does not count the recorded results.** `node apps/docs/build.mjs` at 1e38333 built the site, but the page lists all six result files as \"No: provenance does not match the working tree\" and every verdict as \"not recorded for this revision\", while the command-line checker accepts the same files in release mode from the same tree. The docs build probably changes the tree's fingerprint while it runs (it packs and installs the library) before `apps/docs/lib/conformance.mjs` calls `check()`. For the docs author (a04bfe28413f07034) with the checker's author: reproduce, then either call the checker before the build touches anything or fingerprint what the recordings fingerprint.\n\nOther notes for whoever resumes: the recordings' sidecars say `dirty true` because of the two untracked `bench/history/20261118T03503*` files (left uncommitted on purpose); the release check accepted them. In a strip readout on a plot under about 170 px a click on a line chart does not pin (the pinned row with its buttons does not fit two lines; decision 0004 allows it): candidate for the known-limits page.\n\nMatrix (208c56b): reachable when every cited result passes: 112 pass, 164 weak, 32 pending, 11 cannot be verified here, 257 not applicable; gap list in `wp/matrix-merge3.report.md`. Tests strengthened: Node acceptance (91d649e), browser acceptance with compact area and bar (d936ec3), Node evidence list regenerated (8e73b9f). Checker (3cbf903). Sizes on the packed files: everything 115.74 KiB (target 116), line only 96.81 (target 98).\n\nEngines (author's runs on the shared build of d33df71, `acceptance/results/engines-d33df71/`): acceptance 560 passed on Chrome 158; 399 passed and 161 not run on Playwright Firefox 159 and on Playwright WebKit 27.2 (not Safari); `chart` + `a11y` + `render` 402 passed on Chrome, 388 passed and 15 not run on each other engine; no failure on any engine. The not-run tests need Chrome-only tooling (the accessibility tree through CDP: 151 + 5; multi-touch, pen, heap and listener inspection: the rest) and say so.\n\nMatrix review, Codex gpt-6.2-astra xhigh (observed), `wp/matrix-review.review.md`: of 82 cells that would have read pass, 24 established, 52 weak, 6 not for this revision. Spec WCAG citations corrected after it (6772563). Stacks: owner's \"keep incomplete\" implemented (f3d709b). Docs follow all of it (f43b7a0). Sizes at 9611392 on the packed files: everything 115.74 KiB (target 116), line only 96.81 (target 98).\n\nCoordinator's verification of d33df71 on the shared build built from it (logs `acceptance/results/verify-d33df71/`): core browser suites on SVG 962 passed, 1 skipped (963); `chart` + `a11y` on canvas 312 passed, 4 skipped; `tests/dist` Node 90 of 90; `tests/dist` browser 22 of 26 (the four text tests, fixture out of date); docs browser 169 of 170 (the AX160 page expects a note that the tick-only rule removed). Whole Node suite 1752 passed. Spec rows now cite the WCAG criteria they cover (71f2a02), the coordinator's mapping, to be reviewed with the matrix.\n\nClosing pass commits: lookup (0d9532f), controller (68b3d6c), interaction (d33df71); reports `wp/closing.*.report.md`. The check they answer: `wp/final-check.review.md` (Codex gpt-6.2-sol max, of 9f9eeef). By the owner's stop rule no review follows; the evidence is each fix's own test (written first, failing on the old code) and the coordinator's runs. Verification of 9f9eeef (before the closing pass): SVG 948 passed, 1 skipped; canvas 298 passed, 4 skipped.\n\nFound by the distribution tests (not run since before the interaction rewrite) and fixed in d33df71: a value text too long for one line hid the whole tooltip. Still expected to fail in `tests/dist/text.browser.test.ts`: the scatter fixture's premise that no sentinel x label is truncated is out of date since 6ebdb6f (a scatter's x domain ends on drawn ticks; `wp/closing.layout-labels.report.md`): the acceptance author adjusts the fixture after this run.\n# Release acceptance\n\nFrom the repository root, run the checker tests and record the four Vitest configurations.\n\n```sh\nnode --test acceptance/test/*.test.mjs\nnode acceptance/run.mjs unit\nnode acceptance/run.mjs browser\nnode acceptance/run.mjs dist\nnode acceptance/run.mjs dist-browser\nnode acceptance/check.mjs --release --write --results \\\n  acceptance/results/unit.json acceptance/results/browser.json \\\n  acceptance/results/dist.json acceptance/results/dist-browser.json\n```\n\nBuild the packages before the browser and distribution configurations. Each wrapper run writes Vitest JSON and a neighbouring `*.provenance.json` with the Git commit, dirty flag, tree fingerprint, result hash and hashes of every cited test file. The sidecar records a working-tree change during Vitest and the release checker rejects that run. The generated `acceptance/report.md` does not affect the fingerprint. Supply the result paths to the checker and keep their sidecars beside them. For a filtered run, use `--output` to keep the full configuration’s result intact.\n\nThe checker prints provenance, the matrix and the sources of each result. It writes [report.md](report.md) only with `--write`. Without results, it checks citations and shows static coverage and any cited packed size measurements. An omitted result leaves its assertions unverified. By default the checker can display historical reports, including archived absolute paths that carry a full repository-relative suffix.\n\nUse `--roots <directory>…` for result paths relative to additional roots such as `tests/dist` or `packages/react`. Paths that match more than one cited file are structural errors.\n\nStructural errors exit 1. In `--release` mode, results must have provenance that matches the working tree. Every cell must be `pass` or `not applicable`. An owner can explicitly waive a `not run` or `cannot be verified here` cell by adding `\"waivers\": { \"<chart type>\": { \"reason\": \"…\", \"grantedBy\": \"…\" } }` to its criterion. The waiver appears in the report and leaves the original status visible.\n\n| Status | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| pass | All cited assertions passed and measured targets were met. |\n| fail | A cited assertion or suite failed, or a measured target was exceeded. |\n| weak | Partial evidence leaves a documented gap. |\n| no evidence | The criterion applies and has no relevant evidence. |\n| not run | A cited assertion is missing or skipped, or the cell awaits a valid measurement. |\n| not recorded for this revision | Its only answers come from runs or a size record whose provenance is another tree or pack. |\n| cannot be verified here | The criterion requires an environment or rehearsal outside the supplied runs. |\n| not applicable | The matrix records why the criterion does not apply. |\n\n## Browser engines\n\nThe browser configuration runs on the engine `AXYLO_ENGINE` names: `chromium` (system Chrome, the default), `firefox` or `webkit` (Playwright's own builds; WebKit is the engine, not Safari). Each engine gets its own result file:\n\n```sh\nnode acceptance/run.mjs browser                        # acceptance/results/browser.json\nAXYLO_ENGINE=firefox node acceptance/run.mjs browser   # acceptance/results/browser-firefox.json\nAXYLO_ENGINE=webkit node acceptance/run.mjs browser    # acceptance/results/browser-webkit.json\n```\n\nThe harness records `meta.engine` and `meta.engineVersion` on every test, and the sidecar records the engine asked for and the version the results declare. The checker refuses a result file that mixes engines or disagrees with its sidecar. A browser citation takes an optional `engine` (default `chromium`) and is answered only by results of that engine; a pass on one engine never stands for another. A test that reported itself not run on an engine (`meta.notRun`) is `not run` with that reason. Results without engine records predate the switch and count as Chrome; in a run on another engine, a test without a record (the docs and distribution suites launch Chrome themselves) counts for no engine.\n\n## Reading the checker from code\n\n`check()` (acceptance/check.mjs) returns the Markdown `report` and `table`, and the same content as data: `rows` (per criterion its `id`, `statement` and, per chart type, `status`, `reasons` and `reports`, each with `file`, `label`, `source`, `results`, `engine`, `engineVersion` and `provenanceError`) and `runs`. `STATUSES` and `CHART_TYPES` are exported beside it. acceptance/provenance.mjs exports the configurations (`CONFIGS`, `CONFIGURATIONS`), `ENGINES`, `engineLabel()`, `defaultResultFile()` and `resultFiles()`, the default result file of every configuration and engine.\n\n## Review and cite evidence\n\nUse the spec ID and short wording. Section 3.6 cells use `T-<rule>-<chart type>`. Give every excluded chart type a `notApplicable` reason. Compact covers line, area and bar variants where the rule applies.\n\nUse `evidence` for the checks a cell needs, `gaps` for the unverified part when those checks are incomplete, and `weak` for supporting citations with a specific `reason`. Use `limits` to describe the scope of a result, `cannotVerify` for environmental requirements outside the supplied runs, and `pending` for work awaiting a valid run.\n\nEach citation specifies a repository-relative `file` and a `kind` of `unit`, `property`, `browser`, `size`, `benchmark`, `document` or `manual rehearsal`. A test citation gives its exact Vitest `title` or a `prefix` of a describe title when all reported cases are required. A generated title may include its source template as `registration`. Use `ancestors` to cite a specific suite when titles repeat. The checker refuses citations of `.fails`, `.todo` and `.skip` and requires every matching result to pass.\n\nA suite whose cases the source scan cannot list (an `it.each` over rows computed at run time, such as DX10's per-example renders) is cited by its describe `prefix` with `atLeast`: the citation passes when every reported test under the prefix passed, none was skipped, the titles the scan does know are among them, and at least that many distinct tests passed. The `kind` must fit the file: `browser` for `*.browser.test.*` files, `unit` and `property` for the others. A title the scan finds only through a template, or only under an `if`, `else`, `&&`, `||` or `?:`, needs a run that shows it: a supplied result of its file or the `EVIDENCE.json` beside it. Without either the report lists a warning; a result or `EVIDENCE.json` that covers the file but lacks the title is a structural error. A `size` citation counts only when the record names the tarball integrity of the pack this tree makes (`npm pack`, as `scripts/pack.mjs` packs); the targets are `SIZE_TARGETS` in `scripts/size.mjs`. A run whose provenance sidecar does not match the tree, in any mode, and in a release a run without a sidecar, leaves its cells `not recorded for this revision`.\n\nFor a describe suite shared across chart types, add a chart-specific `casePrefix` and the expected number of distinct `cases`. The checker requires all statically known cases and the expected number of passing current registrations. A failed archived result within that scope still appears in the report. Release checks also require matching provenance.\n\nA `size` citation names the budgeted `entries` in the packed size artifact. Historical benchmarks carry a `workload` and optional `history`. Documents, manual rehearsals and provisional benchmark workloads need separate review.\n\nThe archived four-configuration review includes [review notes](review.json) with the prior status baseline, failure causes and the browser helpers checked by negative controls.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat apps/docs/pages/data.md apps/docs/pages/updating.md'","aggregated_output":"# Your data\n\nAfter this page you can pass the same table as long rows, wide rows or typed columns; say which kind of time your x values are; control what happens to missing values; and give values a unit, a format and a percent scale.\n\n## One table, three shapes\n\nTake four days with two readings per day: sessions for two channels.\n\n| day | Direct | Referral |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2026-03-02 | 3,120 | 410 |\n| 2026-03-03 | 3,380 | 455 |\n| 2026-03-04 | 3,290 | 390 |\n| 2026-03-05 | 3,510 | 520 |\n\nAxylo accepts this table in three shapes. All three produce the same chart, the same table view and the same diagnostics; row numbers in diagnostics and events refer to your own data in the shape you gave it.\n\n### Long rows\n\nOne row per x and series, as most SQL queries and APIs return it. `series` names the field whose value says which series a row belongs to; that value is the series' key and its name.\n\n[example: data-long](../examples/data-long.ts)\n\n### Wide rows\n\nOne row per x, one field per series. `y` is a list of fields, and there is no `series`. Passing both is a type error, and at run time it raises [AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150). Each field is a series: its key is the field name (`direct`), its name for the reader is the field's `label` (`'Direct'`). Use the key in `colors` and in event handlers, and the label for display. The series share one value axis: the first field's `domain`, `scale` and `tickFormat` set it (a `tickFormat` on a later field is reported, [AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150)), and with more than one field the axis is titled \"Value\" (the `value` message). The chart's generated name and its summary name the series rather than that axis (\"Line chart of Visits and Signups by day\", with fields labelled `'Visits'` and `'Signups'`). When the series have different units, the summary gives each series' range in its own unit and no range for the chart as a whole.\n\n[example: data-wide](../examples/data-wide.ts)\n\n### Typed columns\n\n`data: { columns }` with one array-like per field (an array or a typed array), all of the same length; different lengths are error [AX100](diagnostics.md#ax100). In a `Float64Array` or `Float32Array`, `NaN` means \"no value\" and raises nothing. This shape suits large data, because Axylo can read such a column in place; see [what Axylo reads, keeps and copies](data.md#what-axylo-reads-keeps-and-copies).\n\n[example: data-columns](../examples/data-columns.ts)\n\n## Fields\n\nA field is a key of your rows, written as a string, or an object when it needs more:\n\n```ts\nx: 'day'\ny: { field: 'sessions', label: 'Sessions', unit: 'visits' }\n```\n\nThe object form adds a label (the axis title, and the series name in tooltip, table and announcements), a unit, formats, a scale, a missing-value rule and author bounds. The [options reference](options.md#value-fields) lists them all.\n\nWith TypeScript, field names are checked against your row type. A misspelled field, a text field on a value axis, or wide `y` together with `series` does not compile. With untyped rows (from `fetch().json()`, say) the same mistakes are reported at run time: a field that no row has is [AX101](diagnostics.md#ax101), and the message lists the keys the rows do have and, when one is close, the closest match. With no rows, there is nothing to check a field against, and nothing is reported.\n\n## Three kinds of time\n\nDates are where charts most often go wrong without anyone noticing. Axylo keeps three kinds apart and never mixes them silently.\n\n| Kind | Looks like | Means | Shown |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Calendar date | `'2026-02-24'`, `'2026-02'`, `'2026'` | A day, month or year, wherever you are | The same in every time zone |\n| Instant | Epoch milliseconds with `type: 'time'`, a `Date`, `'2026-02-24T09:30:00Z'`, `'2026-02-24T09:30:00+01:00'` | One moment, the same everywhere | In `timeZone` (default: the viewer's zone), with the zone named in the tooltip heading and the summary |\n| Wall-clock time | `'2026-02-24T09:30'` (no offset) | What a clock showed, zone unknown | As written, with hint [AX112](diagnostics.md#ax112) |\n\n### The off-by-one-day trap\n\nA daily metric is a calendar date. Many back ends send it as an instant at UTC midnight: `'2026-02-24T00:00:00Z'`, or the epoch milliseconds of that moment. For a viewer in New York that moment is the evening of 23 February, so every daily value appears one day early. In Berlin it is still the 24th. Showing the instant in the viewer's zone is correct for an instant; the data said \"this moment\", not \"this day\".\n\nThe example below has the same five values sent both ways. Your browser's time zone decides how the first chart reads: west of UTC, its first point is on the 23rd.\n\n[example: dates](../examples/dates.ts)\n\nAxylo notices at least two distinct instants that all fall at the same time of day, whole days apart, and raises hint [AX111](diagnostics.md#ax111). The fix is one of:\n\n- Send calendar dates: `'2026-02-24'`. `new Date(ms).toISOString().slice(0, 10)` turns a UTC midnight into one.\n- Keep the values and declare `x: { field: 'day', type: 'date' }`. Axylo then reads each instant (string, number or `Date`) as the calendar day it falls on in UTC, as the [daily example](getting-started.md#daily-data-five-things-to-get-right) does.\n- If they really are instants and everyone should see UTC, set `x: { field: 'day', type: 'time', timeZone: 'UTC' }`.\n\n### How Axylo decides\n\nWhen `type` is not given, each value of the x field votes for a kind:\n\n- `YYYY-MM-DD`, `YYYY-MM` or `YYYY` strings for a calendar date;\n- date-time strings with `Z` or an offset, and `Date` objects, for an instant;\n- date-time strings without an offset for a wall-clock time;\n- finite numbers for a number. A bare number is never taken for an instant: epoch milliseconds need `type: 'time'`, and numbers that look like timestamps raise hint [AX109](diagnostics.md#ax109);\n- anything else for a category.\n\nCategories win when they are at least as many as all the other votes together. Otherwise the kind with the most votes wins, ties going to date, then instant, then wall-clock time, then number. Values of any other kind are then unusable: they are not drawn, and they raise [AX107](diagnostics.md#ax107) with a note to the reader. Three category strings, two dates and two numbers therefore make a date axis that keeps the two dates. When a feed mixes kinds, declare `type`.\n\nCategories keep the order in which they first appear.\n\n`timeZone` takes `'local'` (the default, the viewer's zone), `'UTC'`, or an IANA zone such as `'Europe/Paris'`. It applies to instants. A zone the browser does not know is [AX152](diagnostics.md#ax152), and the viewer's zone is used.\n\nCalendar periods other than years and months (quarters, ISO weeks) are categories in 1.0: write them as you want them read (`'2025 Q1'`) and set `ordered: true` on the x field so that their labels may be thinned when they do not fit.\n\n## Missing values\n\nAxylo never draws a missing value as zero unless you ask, and does not connect a line across a missing value it knows about. It knows about one when your data says so:\n\n| In your data | Drawn | Reported |\n|---|---|---|\n| `null`, or `''` | A gap | Nothing: this is how to say \"no data\" |\n| `NaN` in a `Float64Array` or `Float32Array` | A gap | Nothing |\n| The value field absent from a row (`undefined`) | A gap | Warning [AX102](diagnostics.md#ax102), and the reader's note |\n| The x or series field absent from a row | The row is not drawn | Warning [AX102](diagnostics.md#ax102), and the reader's note |\n| `NaN` or `Infinity` in rows | A gap | Warning [AX103](diagnostics.md#ax103), and the reader's note |\n| `0` | Zero | Nothing |\n\nA day with **no row at all** is different: the data says nothing about it. Axylo infers that a step is missing only from a series' own rows, when the x axis is dates, instants, wall-clock times or numbers and the series is regular: it has at least three rows, every interval between neighbouring rows is a whole multiple of the smallest one, and at least half of the intervals are the smallest one. A missing step it infers is drawn as a gap, as if the row held `null`, with hint [AX105](diagnostics.md#ax105). It infers nothing before the first row or after the last, in a series of one or two rows, in an irregular series, or when there would be more than twice as many missing steps as rows (and more than 1,000). Another series having a row at that x does not make the step missing in this one: a slot a series simply has no row for (a weekly series beside a daily one) is not a gap, its line is joined across it, and a bar chart draws no bar and no \"no data\" mark there. In a stack it is different, see below. So **when you know a day is missing, send it with `null`**: that is the instruction Axylo always follows.\n\nA value standing alone between two gaps is drawn as a point, so it does not vanish. On a bar chart a missing value gets a small \"no data\" mark (at the baseline, or for a stacked bar at the edge of its stack), distinct from a zero bar. Tooltip, table and announcements say \"No data\". When a warning means values were left out, the chart shows a short note to the reader (\"Values not shown: 3. See table.\") inside the figure, in every build.\n\n**In a stack** (stacked areas or bars), a series with no value at an x is a missing part, whatever the reason: a `null`, an inferred missing step, or no row there at all. The part has zero height, the series' line follows its band, and the total at that x is marked \"incomplete\" in the tooltip, the table and announcements, so a stack never shows a total made of values the data does not contain. A weekly series stacked with a daily one is incomplete on the days between its rows; see [the area chart's example](area.md#edge-case-a-missing-part-of-a-stack).\n\nFor counts, where a day without a row means nothing happened, declare it: `missing: 'zero'` turns null, absent and inferred-missing values of that field into zeros, and the note says the values are shown differently from the data.\n\n[example: missing](../examples/missing.ts)\n\nHere are the values an application sends by mistake, all at once. The note under the chart is what the reader sees; the console has one line per code.\n\n[example: line-dirty](../examples/line-dirty.ts)\n\n## Numbers sent as strings\n\nSome database drivers return counts and decimals as strings (Postgres `bigint` and `numeric`, BigQuery). Axylo checks the whole value field and skips missing values such as `null` and `\"\"` when deciding whether to convert it.\n\nA decimal string has an optional `+` or `-`, then one or more digits from `0` to `9`. A decimal point is optional and must be followed by one or more digits. An optional exponent starts with `e` or `E`, followed by an optional sign and one or more digits. The whole string must fit this grammar, with no whitespace. Axylo accepts `\"40\"`, `\"-1.5\"`, `\"+2\"`, `\"1e3\"` and `\"2.5E-2\"`. It rejects `\".5\"`, `\"5.\"`, `\"0x10\"`, `\"1,234\"`, `\" 40 \"` and `\"1e\"`.\n\n- When every non-missing value matches, Axylo converts the strings to JavaScript numbers. Hint [AX104](diagnostics.md#ax104) asks you to declare `parse: 'number'`. The declaration suppresses that hint and lets TypeScript accept the string field on a value axis.\n- A number, a string outside the grammar, or another kind of value such as `true` prevents string conversion for the whole field, even with `parse: 'number'`. Axylo draws the finite numbers and reports the non-missing strings it leaves out with warning [AX106](diagnostics.md#ax106).\n\nJavaScript represents integers exactly up to 9,007,199,254,740,991 (`Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER`). Larger integers can round without a diagnostic. For example, `\"9007199254740993\"` reads as 9,007,199,254,740,992. When a matching string such as `\"1e999\"` converts to infinity, Axylo leaves it out with warning [AX103](diagnostics.md#ax103). When exact counts could exceed the safe integer limit, check accepted strings with `Number.isSafeInteger(Number(value))` before charting.\n\n[example: numeric-strings](../examples/numeric-strings.ts)\n\n### A field that mixes numbers and strings\n\nWith `[1250, \"1340.5\", 980]` as given, the middle value leaves a break in the line. Its table cell reads “No data”. Axylo reports AX106 and the chart shows “Values not shown: 1. See table.” in the figure and in the accessibility tree.\n\nBefore charting, convert only strings that match the grammar. Leave other values as they came in so Axylo can report them. The second chart draws all three valid values and adds a fourth row with `\"0x10\"` to show how AX106 and the reader's note still report the omission.\n\n[example: mixed-numbers](../examples/mixed-numbers.html)\n\n## Order and duplicates\n\nRows can come in any order. Line, area and scatter charts are drawn in x order, and each value stays with its own row; categories keep the order in which they first appear.\n\n[example: unsorted](../examples/unsorted.ts)\n\nOn a line, area or bar chart one x has one value per series. Two rows with the same x in the same series are error [AX108](diagnostics.md#ax108): that series is not drawn, while the other series are; with no series left to draw, the chart shows its error state and keeps its table. Axylo does not know whether you meant the sum, the last or the average. Two donut slices with the same label are the same error, and the donut is not drawn. On a scatter chart repeated x values, and repeated points, are ordinary data.\n\n## Units, formats and percentages\n\n| Option on a value field | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `label` | The axis title, and the series name in tooltip, table and announcements. Default: the field name. |\n| `unit` | Follows every value in tooltip, table and announcements, and is part of the axis title. |\n| `format` | How values are written in tooltip, table and announcements: `Intl.NumberFormat` options, or a function. Default: the locale's grouping, the full integer part and up to six significant fraction digits (`123,456.75`). |\n| `tickFormat` | How axis ticks are written. Default: the locale's grouping with the fraction digits the tick step needs (`50,000`); compact (`1.2M`) when the largest tick is at least a million; scientific from 10¹⁵. |\n| `scale: 'percent'` | The values are percentages from 0 to 100. |\n| `scale: 'ratio'` | The values are fractions from 0 to 1, shown as percentages. |\n\nA percent or ratio scale is declared, never guessed from a label. Its axis ends at full scale (100%) only when the largest value is at least half of full scale and not above it; otherwise it ends at the first tick at or above the data, so 4% is not drawn as a sliver of 100%. Numbers and dates are formatted for the `locale` option if given, else the document's language (`<html lang>`), else the browser's. A formatter that fails (a function that throws, or `Intl` options that `Intl` rejects) is warning [AX160](diagnostics.md#ax160), and the default format is used for that value.\n\n[example: units](../examples/units.ts)\n\nBar and area value axes always include zero, since a bar's length and an area's height are their values. A `domain` that leaves zero out is widened, with warning [AX110](diagnostics.md#ax110). Line and scatter axes fit the data.\n\nValue axes (y, and both axes of a scatter chart) end on a drawn tick at or beyond the data, the reference lines and bands, and stack totals; in a plot too narrow to label an end tick, the axis takes the next coarser step. A date, time or number x axis of a line, area or bar chart spans exactly its data instead (for bars, plus half a band at each end), so a time series is not padded to the next round tick.\n\n## What Axylo reads, keeps and copies\n\n- **Rows** are read when the chart is created or updated, and never changed. Their values are copied into Axylo's own typed arrays. Events hand back your own row object (`point.row`), and diagnostics name rows by their index in your data. A position no row of yours holds (an inferred missing day, a donut's \"Other\") has `row: null` and `index: -1`; check before you look a row up.\n- **Typed columns** of values (`Float64Array`, `Float32Array`) are read in place, without a copy, when Axylo can: the x values are already in order, no missing step is inferred, and no value has to change (no `Infinity`, no `missing: 'zero'` to apply). Otherwise Axylo copies what it needs; grouping long columns into series and scatter charts can need new storage too. Either way, do not change an array after passing it: to change the data, pass new arrays with `update({ data })`.\n- **Appended rows** are read once and added to the chart's own storage; `keep` limits how much is kept. See [Updating, streaming and zoom](updating.md#append-and-keep).\n\n## Checking data before you draw\n\n`validate(type, options)` runs the chart's input checks without a page. It returns every finding from those checks, including hints, in every build. For a check you can run with `node check.mjs`, see [Diagnostics](diagnostics.md#validate-in-node-and-in-tests).\n# Updating, streaming and zoom\n\nAfter this page you can change a chart's options and data, feed it a live stream, set and follow its zoom, listen to its events and remove it, and you know the rules that keep every one of these calls predictable.\n\nA chart function returns a handle with six members:\n\n```ts\nconst chart = line('#sessions', options);\n\nchart.update({ title: 'Sessions' });                                   // change options\nchart.append([{ day: '2026-05-01', sessions: 41210 }], { keep: 120 }); // add rows\nchart.zoom(['2026-03-01', '2026-03-31']);                               // set the visible x range\nconst off = chart.on('hover', point => console.log(point));            // listen; off() stops\nchart.diagnostics;                                                     // what is wrong, right now\nchart.destroy();                                                       // remove the chart\n```\n\nThere is no `resize()`: the chart follows its container by itself.\n\n## The rules every call follows\n\n1. **Calls apply in the order you make them.** `update({ data })` followed by `append(rows)` appends to the new data.\n2. **Checking is immediate.** Each call reads and checks its input before it returns. `chart.diagnostics` is up to date as soon as the call returns, and a `strict` chart throws from that call.\n3. **Drawing waits for the next frame.** All the calls made before the next animation frame are drawn once, together.\n4. **Everything switches together.** Marks, labels, the tooltip, keyboard navigation and the accessibility text move to the new state in the same frame. No frame shows half of one state and half of another.\n\n## update: change options\n\n`update(patch)` takes any options of the chart's type. For each key:\n\n| In the patch | Effect |\n|---|---|\n| Present with a value | Replaces the old value whole. There is no deep merge: `y: { field: 'revenue' }` replaces the whole `y`, label and unit included. |\n| Present as `undefined` | Returns that option to its default. |\n| Absent | Stays as it is. |\n\nA new `data` replaces the data, including anything appended. Changing only how values look (`domain`, `format`, `tickFormat`, `title`, `colors`) does not make the chart read the rows again; changing a field, its `label` or its `unit` does.\n\n[example: update](../examples/update.html)\n\n## append and keep\n\n`append(rows, { keep })` adds rows at the end.\n\n- When the new rows are at or after the last x, they are read and added without reading the earlier rows again. Rows before the last x are still accepted, but the chart rebuilds its data from all the rows it holds, which is slower, and raises warning [AX151](diagnostics.md#ax151); a `strict` chart rejects such a call instead.\n- `keep` is the number of distinct x values to retain; older ones are dropped. Without it, every row is kept.\n- When the view is zoomed to the latest edge, it moves with new data, keeping its width. Zoomed elsewhere, it stays where it is.\n- Appended rows are checked like any others, and a `strict` chart throws on a bad one.\n- Arriving data is not announced. The plot's value text, which describes the point in keyboard focus, is not rewritten when an append only changes the point count or position number; it is rewritten when the focused point's own values change or the point leaves the window. Whether a screen reader then speaks depends on the reader; this has not been tried with one.\n\n[example: stream](../examples/stream.html)\n\n### Pause and resume\n\nOnce a chart has been appended to, its toolbar shows a Pause control. While paused, new rows are taken and checked but not shown, and the view does not move, so a reader can study a moment of a live chart. Their diagnostics come with the `append` call, as when running: rows that bring a ninth series raise [AX130](diagnostics.md#ax130) then, and a `strict` chart rejects that call. Resume shows the data as it is then: everything that arrived, within `keep`. Pause is in the chart's own toolbar for the reader; there is no method for it.\n\n## zoom\n\n`zoom([from, to])` sets the visible x range, given in the form of the x axis: date strings or `Date` objects for calendar dates, `Date` objects or epoch milliseconds for instants, numbers for a number axis. `zoom(null)` shows everything. A range that cannot be read is reported and ignored; `zoom` never throws.\n\nZoom applies to the date, time and number x axes of full line, area, bar and scatter charts (scatter along x only in 1.0). A category axis, a donut and a compact chart do not zoom in 1.0. There `zoom()` changes nothing and reports [AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150) when it is called: the diagnostic is printed and passed to `onDiagnostic`, and is not kept in `chart.diagnostics`. A compact chart does not keep the range for later either, so turning `compact` off does not apply it. On a scatter chart the whole range is the x axis as drawn, which ends on ticks (spec F7): in a narrow plot that axis can take a coarser step and a range wider than the data, and Zoom out and Reset zoom return to that range.\n\nWhere zoom applies, the reader has four ways to zoom:\n\n1. **Buttons, with single clicks.** Zoom in and Zoom out in the toolbar; once zoomed, also Pan left and Pan right (half a view each way, unavailable at an end) and Reset zoom.\n2. **Choosing a range.** Drag across the plot. Or, without dragging, press Select range (the symbol after Zoom in) and click or tap the two ends of the range, in either order; the first end is marked in the plot. While Select range is pressed, a click sets an end and neither pins a tooltip nor selects. The toggle releases itself after the second end; pressing it again or Escape releases it sooner, and a drag zooms as a drag and releases it too.\n3. **Keys,** while the plot has focus: `+` and `-` zoom, `0` shows everything, and Shift with Left or Right pans. The wheel zooms as well while the plot has focus, never with Ctrl or Meta held, so browser zoom and page scrolling keep working.\n4. **Pinch,** when `zoom: 'touch'` is set: two fingers zoom, and moving them pans.\n\nA one-finger drag always scrolls the page. `zoom: false` removes the reader's ways to zoom.\n\n## on: events\n\n`on(event, listener)` returns a function that removes the listener.\n\n| Event | Payload | When |\n|---|---|---|\n| `hover` | A point, or `null` when nothing is picked | The datum under the pointer, or the keyboard's point, changes. |\n| `select` | A point | The reader clicks a point or presses Enter or Space on it. |\n| `zoom` | `[from, to]`, or `null` for the whole range | A `zoom()` call, or the reader's zoom or pan, changes the visible range. Not when the view moves with appended data. |\n\nA point is `{ series, x, y, row, index }`:\n\n- `series` is the series key: the `series` field's value for long rows, the field name for wide rows (not its `label`), the slice label on a donut;\n- `x` is in the form `zoom()` accepts for the axis: a `Date` for an instant, `'YYYY-MM-DD'` for a calendar date, an ISO date-time without offset for a wall-clock time, the category's label, otherwise the number. The two ends of a `zoom` event take the same form, except that an end between two calendar dates stays a number, which `zoom()` reads as the same position. Either can be passed to another chart's `zoom()` unchanged. Do not turn a wall-clock time into a `Date`: that would read it in the viewer's zone;\n- `y` is the value, `null` for no data;\n- `row` is your own row object (with typed columns, an object built from the columns at that index), and `index` its index in your data. A position no row of yours holds (an inferred missing step, a donut's \"Other\") has `row: null` and `index: -1`.\n\n[example: zoom](../examples/zoom.html)\n\nA zoom event's range drives a second chart:\n\n[example: zoom-linked](../examples/zoom-linked.html)\n\n## diagnostics\n\n`chart.diagnostics` lists the diagnostics for the current options and data. It is current as soon as a create, `update` or `append` returns. Diagnostics found later, while drawing (contrast, labels that stop fitting after a resize, a formatter that fails), are added when found. See [Diagnostics](diagnostics.md).\n\n## destroy\n\n`destroy()` cancels the pending frame, disconnects every observer and listener, removes what the chart added to its element, and makes later calls do nothing. Call it when the chart's element leaves your page; a new chart can then be created on the same element (see [using Axylo in a framework](getting-started.md#using-axylo-in-a-framework)). A chart whose element leaves the page without `destroy()` lets go of the page's listeners until the element returns.\n\n## Strict mode\n\nWith `strict: true`, any error or warning found while checking a create, `update` or `append` call throws from that call, and the chart keeps its last valid state. The thrown error is named `AxyloStrictError`, its message starts with the first diagnostic, and its `diagnostics` property has all of them. A rejected create leaves nothing behind in the page.\n\nHints never throw. Neither do diagnostics found later while drawing, nor an internal failure while drawing ([AX199](diagnostics.md#ax199)): those are reported when found. A failure while the call itself is checked (a row getter that throws) rejects the call.\n\n[example: strict](../examples/strict.html)\n\nStrict mode suits tests and development builds, where a bad row should stop you. In production a chart that shows a gap and tells the reader is usually better than one that throws.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,91p' docs/02-product-spec.md && sed -n '228,299p' docs/02-product-spec.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,91p' docs/02-product-spec.md && sed -n '228,299p' docs/02-product-spec.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Phase 1b: Axylo product specification\n\nRevision 3, 2026-11-18. Revised after three independent reviews ([ledger](../reviews/2026-11-18/ledger.md)), then updated with the owner's decisions of the same day: scatter is in 1.0, a compact variant is in 1.0, and the mark renderer is decided by measurement in M1.\n\nBased on the findings in [01-landscape.md](01-landscape.md). Finding IDs (R1–R16 for readers, D1–D10 for developers) refer to that document. Criterion IDs defined here are F, L, I, A (reader), DX (developer) and P (performance).\n\n## 1. What Axylo is\n\n**Axylo is the everyday chart you can ship without checking it.** The smallest correct call produces a chart that represents the data faithfully, stays readable at any width and in any color scheme, and works with a keyboard and a screen reader. When the data is wrong, Axylo says so, to the developer and to the reader, instead of drawing a guess.\n\nIt is a framework-neutral TypeScript library under the MIT license. 1.0 ships no framework adapter (owner's decision, 2026-11-18): adapters for React, Vue, Svelte and others come later; in a framework, create the chart when its element exists and call `destroy()` when it goes away.\n\nSpeed is a constraint, not the headline: Axylo must stay within the budgets in section 5 with default options. Whether it can do that at a million points with its accessibility layer on is something the first milestone measures, not something this document can promise.\n\n### 1.1 Why someone would choose it\n\nPhase 1 found no library among the ten that holds these together. Each row is the reason to choose Axylo over the library a team would otherwise reach for. The right-hand column is a design target, not a measured result.\n\n| If you would otherwise use | You get today | Axylo aims to add |\n|---|---|---|\n| Chart.js | Simple API, permissive license, widest reach | Keyboard and screen-reader access without author work; reduced-motion support; a tooltip that appears wherever the pointer is; large data without expert options; a stated time zone on every instant |\n| Recharts | The React default | A palette, a title, labels that fit, formatted numbers and dates without code; 100,000 points without seconds of blocking; the same chart outside React |\n| Highcharts | The best reader experience of the ten | The same class of accessibility with no module to import and no commercial license; reduced-motion support; errors that name the field and the row |\n| ECharts or ApexCharts | Many chart types | Fewer types, each of which fits its labels, reports bad data and is accessible by default |\n| uPlot | The speed reference | Automatic sizing, a palette, bars and donuts without path builders, and an accessibility layer, at a speed the budgets in section 5 keep honest |\n\nWhat Axylo does not try to win: number of chart types, visual customization depth, or exploratory analysis.\n\n### 1.2 Who it is for\n\nA developer adding charts to a product or an internal dashboard: data arrives as rows from an API, the chart is one component among many on the page, and the person writing it is not a visualization specialist and will not test it with a screen reader. Coding agents writing that code are the same audience: they benefit from typed fields, one logical data model and diagnostics that say what to change.\n\nNot the audience for 1.0: analysts exploring data in notebooks, designers building bespoke graphics, and teams that need maps, financial charts or network diagrams.\n\n### 1.3 Principles\n\n| Principle | In practice |\n|---|---|\n| Correct before configurable | A default that can mislead (truncated bars, interpolated gaps, a silently dropped label) is a bug, even if an option fixes it. |\n| Say it, do not guess | Anything Axylo cannot draw faithfully becomes a diagnostic with a code, a location and a fix. If values were left out, the reader is told too. |\n| The data is the authority | Exact values, the table, keyboard navigation and descriptions come from the normalized data. The drawing is derived from it and may be simplified; nothing else may be. |\n| Everything a reader needs is on; nothing else is | Title, units, legend, tooltip, keyboard, description and table are on. Entry animation, a marker on every point of a dense line, and decorative toolbars are not. |\n| One way to do each thing | One logical data model, one method per kind of change, one place for colors (CSS). |\n| The page is the theme | Font, text color and color scheme come from the page. |\n\n## 2. Scope of the first release\n\n### 2.1 Chart types\n\n| Type | Variants | Typical question | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Line | Multi-series, gaps; time, date, numeric or category x | How did it change? | 1.0 |\n| Bar | Vertical, horizontal, grouped, stacked | How do categories compare? | 1.0 |\n| Area | Single, stacked | How did the total and its parts change? | 1.0 |\n| Donut | Up to eight slices | What share does each part have? | 1.0 |\n| Scatter | Optional series | How are two measures related? | 1.0 (owner's decision) |\n| Compact variant | Of line, area and bar: marks only, for KPI tiles and sparklines | Which way is it going? | 1.0 (owner's decision) |\n\nPlus, on every cartesian chart: reference lines and bands (a target, an incident window).\n\nThe Phase 1 harness exercised line, bar and donut in all ten libraries (donut in nine; uPlot has none). Area and scatter are added on judgment; Phase 1 found no usage data by chart type. Two reviewers argued for fewer types and one for a compact variant; the owner decided on all five types plus the compact variant. Scatter alone needs two-dimensional hit testing and its own navigation model, so it is built last and carries the most schedule risk.\n\nEach type has its own rules for picking, navigation and limits. They are in the type matrix in section 3.6, and a type does not ship until its row is fully tested.\n\n### 2.2 Workflows\n\n| # | Workflow | What it requires of Axylo |\n|---|---|---|\n| W1 | Show a query result | Rows in, chart out; field types inferred where that is safe; bad rows reported. |\n| W2 | Fill a dashboard | Dozens of charts per page; container-sized; themed by the page; loading, empty and error states; a chart can take its name from the card's own heading. |\n| W3 | Compare categories | Long labels; horizontal bars; grouped and stacked. |\n| W4 | Watch a live metric | Append without redraw cost growing; a pause control; no announcement flood. |\n| W5 | Explore a range | Tooltip anywhere, legend toggle, zoom and pan by single click, drag, touch and keyboard. |\n| W6 | Read it another way | Keyboard, screen reader, table view, high contrast, reduced motion, 400% zoom, color-vision deficiency. |\n\n### 2.3 Supported environments\n\n| Dimension | 1.0 |\n|---|---|\n| Browsers | The current and previous major versions of Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari (macOS and iOS). |\n| TypeScript | 5.4 and later. Plain JavaScript is fully supported; types are an aid, not a requirement. |\n| Frameworks | None needed. No adapter ships in 1.0; the library is called from any framework's mount and unmount hooks. |\n| Text direction | Left-to-right pages. Right-to-left text renders, but the layout is not mirrored; this is stated in the conformance table. |\n| Transformed ancestors | A chart inside a translated or scaled ancestor (any positive scale, uniform or not) is exact. Inside a rotated, skewed or mirrored ancestor it draws, but pointer picking and tooltip placement are wrong; this is not supported in 1.0 and the documentation says so (decision 0004, rule 10). |\n| Built-in text | English, overridable (A10). |\n\n## 3. Acceptance criteria: the reader's experience\n\nEvery criterion maps to at least one test in the acceptance matrix kept in the repository (criterion → test → chart types it applies to → result). \"Property test\" means the statement is checked over at least 1,000 generated datasets. Expected values come from the fixture data and this specification, never from the implementation's output.\n\n### 3.1 Faithful representation\n| DX6 | Colors, font and spacing are CSS custom properties with documented names; the chart inherits `font-family` and `color`. `colors` pins a color to a series name so the same series has the same color on every chart. There is no theme object and no positional palette option. | Theming tests, including a 24-tile page where series subsets differ | R10 |\n| DX7 | Zero runtime dependencies (reconsidered only with a measured comparison). Size targets for 1.0, set by the owner on 2026-11-18 after measurement: 116 KiB gzipped for everything and 98 KiB for a page that uses only `line`, CSS included. (History: the first targets, 40 and 25 KiB, were written before anything was built; 96 and 80 were set the same day from a first analysis, `reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/size.analysis.md`; the correctness passes that followed added about 11 KiB, and a second measured analysis, `reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/size2.analysis.md`, showed 96 and 80 can only be met by making streaming an opt-in import, a public API change the owner left for after 1.0. That change, worth about 15 KiB, is the first size item after 1.0.) | size-limit in CI on the packed distribution | D4 |\n| DX8 | Importing in Node without a DOM does not throw, and `validate()` runs there. Creating, destroying and creating again on the same element works and leaks nothing (the sequence a framework's strict or development mode produces). Works under a Content Security Policy without `unsafe-inline` or `unsafe-eval`, served with real headers. Labels and formatter output are inserted as text, never as HTML. A target that matches no element throws an error that names the selector. | CI tests | D1 |\n| DX9 | Semantic versioning. A deprecated API keeps working for at least one minor release and six months. At most one major version a year. The previous release's documented examples still compile and run against each release candidate. | Release policy; API report diff; upgrade test | D6 |\n| DX10 | Every runnable example in the documentation is compiled and rendered in CI from the packed distribution. | Docs build | D1 |\n| DX11 | MIT license for everything, including zoom and accessibility. Third-party notices are verified at release. | Repository; release check | D7 |\n| DX12 | A coding agent gets the first chart right. Given fixed prompts and trap fixtures (UTC-midnight ISO dates, numeric strings, unsorted rows, missing days, a misspelled field), an agent with only the published docs produces a chart with no error diagnostics that passes the reader criteria. Recorded as agent rehearsal, not as user evidence. | Scripted rehearsal at each release | D1, 1.2 |\n\n## 5. Performance budgets\n\nThese numbers are provisional. They are design targets taken from Phase 1's order-of-magnitude measurements, and they become release gates only after the benchmark harness built in M0 has measured a working Axylo slice in M1 and the limits have been recalibrated and frozen (plan, M1 and M6). Each budget names its workload, metric and statistic so that two people measuring the same build get the same verdict.\n\n| ID | Workload | Metric | Statistic | Provisional limit | Phase 1 reference |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| P1 | `trend` (3×120), default options, reference desktop | Main-thread task time from the call until the chart is complete | Median of 20 runs | 10 ms | Not comparable across libraries below about 35 ms with the Phase 1 method |\n| P2 | One series, 100,000 and 1,000,000 points, typed columns | Same | Median of 20 | 60 ms and 250 ms | See 01, section 5.3: uPlot and tuned Chart.js are the fastest measured |\n| P3 | 600-point window, one appended point per display frame at 60 Hz, driven by an independent clock | Main-thread task time per update (per run: task time in the window divided by the number of updates); and frames later than 25 ms | Median over runs; count per 300 frames | 2 ms; 0 | See 01, section 5.3 |\n| P4 | Pointer moved across a 100,000-point chart | Time from input to the frame showing the matching readout | 95th percentile | One frame (16.7 ms at 60 Hz) | Not measured in Phase 1 |\n| P5 | 24 `trend` charts created on one page, each tile 340 × 200 px | Total main-thread task time | Median of 20 | 200 ms | Not measured in Phase 1 |\n| P6 | Download | Minified and gzipped bytes, CSS included | Exact | DX7 | 01, section 1.4 |\n| P8 | 40 compact charts created on one page, each 160 × 48 px with one series | Total main-thread task time | Median of 20 | 80 ms | Not measured in Phase 1 |\n| P7 | Memory | Bytes owned by Axylo beyond the caller's columns for 1,000,000 points; heap trend over 1,000 create–destroy cycles | Measured after forced collection | At most 1.1× the size of the caller's two columns; no upward slope. (1.1× since 2026-11-18, the owner's decision: once a chart opened on typed columns is appended to, exact x, exact y and one state byte per point are 17 bytes per point against the caller's 16, measured 17,069,760 bytes for 1,000,000 points. Staying under 1× would need segmented columns, a contract change that was not judged worth it for 1.0.) | Not measured in Phase 1 |\n\nThree rules hold whatever the numbers become:\n\n1. Budgets apply to default options. Finding D8 showed libraries that are fast only for authors who know which options to set.\n2. Budgets are absolute, on named hardware profiles. Comparison libraries, uPlot above all, are measured in the same runs and published beside Axylo, but a ratio to another library is not a gate: it would reward leaving out work that library does not do.\n3. The accessibility layer is on in every measured configuration.\n\n## 6. Decisions and the findings behind them\n\n\"Status\" records what the reviews changed.\n\n| Decision | What decided it | Status after review |\n|---|---|---|\n| Diagnostics are a first-class feature with codes | R5: none of the ten named the field, the row or the problem. | Kept. Severity and delivery rewritten (3.5): readers are told too, and production builds are not silent. |\n| Calendar dates, instants and wall-clock times are different types | R3: given instants, six libraries show a daily value on a different day in some zones, which is correct for instants and wrong for dates; given date strings, most of them get it right. The gap is that nothing says which one the author meant. | Kept, sharpened. The test now separates the types, and a hint fires when instants look like dates. Default zone for instants: the viewer's, with the zone always visible. |\n| Accessibility is in the core, on by default | R11–R13. | Kept. The table is built on demand, not eagerly. |\n| The normalized data, not the drawing, is the source for values, table, keyboard and summary | Reviews: a simplified drawing cannot return the values it dropped. | New. |\n| No animation on first render | Reading starts at first paint, and with 24 charts loading at once motion competes with it. R14: four of seven animating libraries ignore reduced motion. | Kept, with the rationale restated. Update transitions stay capped at 200 ms. |\n| Label fitting is a tested invariant with a visible fallback | R2. | Changed. The absolute \"everything always fits\" was infeasible in a 240 px tile; it is now \"nothing overlaps, and nothing disappears without the reader being told\". Rotation comes before truncation. |\n| One logical table, three input forms | D2. | Changed from \"one data shape\": typed columns and wide rows are first-class. |\n| Three methods with a commit contract | D3. | Changed from \"one update method\": `append` and `zoom` were always there, and their ordering is now specified. |\n| Canvas marks with DOM text | D9: a canvas library is the speed reference. D10: one DOM node per point is slow. | **Reopened.** Phase 1 does not show that SVG is slow, only that one node per point is; a single SVG path per series was fast. M1 builds both mark renderers on the same scene and measures them (section 8). Canvas is the working assumption. |\n| Simplified drawing that preserves extremes, on by default | D8. | Kept for lines and areas, with the guarantee restated per pixel column and per run of valid samples. \"Every spike survives\" was wrong as written. |\n| Palette held to measurable thresholds | R7. | Kept as a screen, marked provisional. Marks carry a non-color cue in every mode, which the first draft did not require. |\n| Colors and fonts from CSS | R10. | Kept. Repaint triggers specified; `palette` option replaced by `colors` keyed by series name. |\n| Numeric strings | R5. | Changed: a whole column of plain decimal strings is parsed with a hint; mixed columns are not. |\n| Chart types | Judgment, then the owner's decision. | All five in 1.0, plus the compact variant (3.7). |\n| MIT, including zoom and accessibility | D7. | Kept. |\n| React adapter in 1.0 | Recharts leads npm installs among chart-level libraries. | Built early to test the core API, reviewed twice, then removed from 1.0 by the owner on 2026-11-18 to keep the first release plain TypeScript. Its last state is at commit dac5664 (`packages/react`), the starting point when adapters for React, Vue and Svelte are added. |\n| Budgets apply to defaults | D8. | Kept. The ratio to uPlot is no longer a gate. |\n| Zero runtime dependencies | D4. | Kept provisionally; time-zone arithmetic uses `Intl`. |\n\n## 7. Changes this revision rejected\n\n| Proposal | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| Test with screen-reader users, outside developers or design partners before release | One-person project with no participants. Recorded as a stated limit (3.8). Agent rehearsal (DX12) is used and labelled as such; the developer's screen-reader rehearsal (A3) was dropped from 1.0 by the owner and is stated as not done. |\n| Collect chart sizes and device profiles from beta deployments | A chart library should not phone home. Workload sizes come from the spec and from issue reports. |\n| Switch to horizontal bars automatically when labels do not fit | Changing orientation changes what the chart says at a glance; Axylo suggests it and tells the reader what is hidden. |\n| Remove the first-chart line-count criterion | Kept as a floor, and joined by DX12, which tests getting it right. |\n\n## 8. Decisions still open\n\nSettled by the owner on 2026-11-18: scatter is in 1.0; the compact variant is in 1.0; the mark renderer stays open until M1 measures both.\n\n| Question | Recommendation | Settled by |\n|---|---|---|\n| Canvas or SVG paths for marks? | Decide from the M1 comparison: P1, P2, P3, P5, bytes, memory, theme toggle, forced colors, print. | M1 record. |\n| Which keyboard and screen-reader pattern for the plot? | Decide from the M1 spike: three candidate patterns on the `trend` chart, rehearsed on the five pairs. | M1 record, confirmed in M4. |\n| Is the palette that meets A5 one people want to look at? | Hand-tune from the search output with the thresholds in CI. | M5. |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
