{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n '\"'^#|''^'\"\\\\| AX|readout' docs/02-product-spec.md | cut -c1-500\"","aggregated_output":"1:# Phase 1b: Axylo product specification\n7:## 1. What Axylo is\n15:### 1.1 Why someone would choose it\n29:### 1.2 Who it is for\n35:### 1.3 Principles\n46:## 2. Scope of the first release\n48:### 2.1 Chart types\n65:### 2.2 Workflows\n76:### 2.3 Supported environments\n87:## 3. Acceptance criteria: the reader's experience\n91:### 3.1 Faithful representation\n100:| F6 | Numbers and dates are formatted for the page locale. Ticks and readouts are formatted separately: ticks may be compact (`50K`); tooltip, table and announcements show the full integer part, and the fraction with up to six significant digits not counting the fraction's leading zeros (`123,456.75`; `0.000123456`; `0.1 + 0.2` reads `0.3`), with the unit. A custom formatter that throws falls back to the default and raises a diagnostic. | Expected strings for chosen values (large integers, \n104:| F10 | Stacks place positive and negative values on their own sides of zero, and the axis covers both totals. The readout's total is the sum when every component has the same sign; when signs are mixed it gives the positive total and the negative total, the two ends the reader sees, not their net. A missing component is drawn with zero height and the stack is marked \"incomplete\" in tooltip, table and announcements. A component is missing whenever its series has no value at that x, whatever \n108:### 3.2 Legibility\n121:### 3.3 Interaction\n125:| I1 | Pointing anywhere in the plot shows what is there, by the picking rule for the chart type (section 3.6), within one frame at the 95th percentile. On line, area and bar the tooltip lists every visible series at the nearest x; a series with no row at that x reads \"No observation\", distinct from \"No data\". The tooltip stays inside the viewport, does not cover the active mark or any control, can itself be hovered, and lists at most eight rows plus \"+N more\". In a plot too small for it (de\n132:### 3.4 Accessibility\n149:### 3.5 Diagnostic registry\n155:| AX100 | Error | Typed columns have different lengths | Error state; the message names the columns and their lengths |\n156:| AX101 | Error | A named field is not a key of any row | Error state; the message lists the keys that exist and the closest match |\n157:| AX102 | Warning | A field is absent (`undefined`) in some rows | Gaps |\n158:| AX103 | Warning | A value is `NaN` or infinite | Gap |\n159:| AX104 | Hint | Every non-missing value of a value field is a plain decimal string (`'40'`, `'-1.5'`) | Parsed and drawn; the message suggests `parse: 'number'`. An empty string counts as missing |\n160:| AX105 | Hint | Steps are absent on a regular time, date, wall-clock or number axis | Gaps. When a series would need more absent steps than twice its observations (and more than 1,000), it is treated as irregular instead: no gaps are created and the hint says so |\n161:| AX106 | Warning | A value field mixes numbers with strings, or holds strings that are not plain decimals (`'1,234'`, `'n/a'`) | The strings are not drawn |\n162:| AX107 | Warning | A date or time cannot be parsed | That row is not drawn |\n163:| AX108 | Error | Duplicate x within a series, or duplicate donut labels | That series, or the donut, is not drawn |\n164:| AX109 | Hint | Numbers on an axis declared `time` look like seconds, or a numeric x looks like epoch milliseconds without `type: 'time'` | As declared |\n165:| AX110 | Warning | An author domain on bar or area excluded zero | Domain widened to zero |\n166:| AX111 | Hint | Instants look like calendar dates | As instants, zone shown |\n167:| AX112 | Hint | Date-time strings have no offset | As written |\n168:| AX120 | Error | Negative donut value | Error state |\n169:| AX121 | Warning | More than eight donut slices | Seven largest plus \"Other (n)\" |\n170:| AX130 | Warning | More than eight series | All series, with dash or marker differences |\n171:| AX140 | Warning | No `title` and no `labelledBy` | Generated name |\n172:| AX141 | Warning | Palette below 3:1 on the resolved background | As themed |\n173:| AX142 | Hint | Plot too small to draw | Summary and table control |\n174:| AX143 | Hint | Labels hidden after every remedy | Note shown to the reader |\n175:| AX150 | Warning | Unknown option key | Ignored; the message suggests the closest known key |\n176:| AX151 | Warning | `append` rows out of order | Accepted through the slower full update |\n177:| AX131 | Hint | A compact chart has more than one series | Drawn, with dash differences |\n178:| AX132 | Hint | More bars than pixels along the category axis | Drawn; the message suggests aggregating |\n179:| AX133 | Hint | A scatter chart has more than 50,000 points | Drawn; the message says drawing may be slow |\n180:| AX134 | Error | Series × x positions exceeds 20,000,000 (long rows with many series and little shared x) | Error state; the message gives both counts and suggests fewer series or a scatter chart |\n181:| AX152 | Warning | `timeZone` is not a zone the browser knows | The viewer's zone is used |\n182:| AX160 | Warning | A custom formatter threw | The default format is used for that value. A value formatter that throws changes how a value reads, so the reader's note (F12) is shown; a tick formatter that throws only changes an axis label, which is not a value left out or changed, so it is reported without a note (and so the note can never change which ticks fit) |\n183:| AX199 | Error | An error was thrown while Axylo was building the chart: by Axylo itself, or by code the author supplied that Axylo was running (a row getter, a callback; a formatter that throws is AX160, and the default format is used) | Error state with a generic message; the error is passed to `onDiagnostic` and printed |\n189:### 3.6 Type matrix\n201:### 3.7 Compact variant\n215:### 3.8 What the evidence will and will not show\n219:## 4. Acceptance criteria: the developer's experience\n236:## 5. Performance budgets\n245:| P4 | Pointer moved across a 100,000-point chart | Time from input to the frame showing the matching readout | 95th percentile | One frame (16.7 ms at 60 Hz) | Not measured in Phase 1 |\n257:## 6. Decisions and the findings behind them\n282:## 7. Changes this revision rejected\n291:## 8. Decisions still open\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,39p' apps/docs/pages/data.md && rg -n 'readout|compact|[Cc]olour scheme' apps/docs/pages/accessibility.md apps/docs/pages/theming.md | cut -c1-680 && sed -n '73,100p' bench/size-latest.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,39p' apps/docs/pages/data.md && rg -n 'readout|compact|[Cc]olour scheme' apps/docs/pages/accessibility.md apps/docs/pages/theming.md | cut -c1-680 && sed -n '73,100p' bench/size-latest.json\"","aggregated_output":"# Your data\n\nAfter this page you can pass the same table as long rows, wide rows or typed columns; say which kind of time your x values are; control what happens to missing values; and give values a unit, a format and a percent scale.\n\n## One table, three shapes\n\nTake four days with two readings per day: sessions for two channels.\n\n| day | Direct | Referral |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2026-03-02 | 3,120 | 410 |\n| 2026-03-03 | 3,380 | 455 |\n| 2026-03-04 | 3,290 | 390 |\n| 2026-03-05 | 3,510 | 520 |\n\nAxylo accepts this table in three shapes. All three produce the same chart, the same table view and the same diagnostics; row numbers in diagnostics and events refer to your own data in the shape you gave it.\n\n### Long rows\n\nOne row per x and series, as most SQL queries and APIs return it. `series` names the field whose value says which series a row belongs to; that value is the series' key and its name.\n\n[example: data-long](../examples/data-long.ts)\n\n### Wide rows\n\nOne row per x, one field per series. `y` is a list of fields, and there is no `series`. Passing both is a type error, and at run time it raises [AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150). Each field is a series: its key is the field name (`direct`), its name for the reader is the field's `label` (`'Direct'`). Use the key in `colors` and in event handlers, and the label for display. The series share one value axis: the first field's `domain`, `scale` and `tickFormat` set it (a `tickFormat` on a later field is reported, [AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150)), and with more than one field the axis is titled \"Value\" (the `value` message). The chart's generated name and its summary name the series rather than that axis (\"Line chart of Visits and Signups by day\", with fields labelled `'Visits'` and `'Signups'`). When the series have different units, the summary gives each series' range in its own unit and no range for the chart as a whole.\n\n[example: data-wide](../examples/data-wide.ts)\n\n### Typed columns\n\n`data: { columns }` with one array-like per field (an array or a typed array), all of the same length; different lengths are error [AX100](diagnostics.md#ax100). In a `Float64Array` or `Float32Array`, `NaN` means \"no value\" and raises nothing. This shape suits large data, because Axylo can read such a column in place; see [what Axylo reads, keeps and copies](data.md#what-axylo-reads-keeps-and-copies).\n\n[example: data-columns](../examples/data-columns.ts)\n\n## Fields\n\nA field is a key of your rows, written as a string, or an object when it needs more:\n\napps/docs/pages/accessibility.md:11:| Assistive technology | The figure is a group with an accessible name (`title`, or the heading named by `labelledBy`, or a generated name) and a description: the generated summary of type, series, x range with its time zone, value range, each series' minimum, maximum and last value, and anything left out. A compact chart is an image with the same name and description. The plot is one native range input whose value text describes the point in focus: its x, the value of each visible series with its unit, and its position (\"Point 4 of 120\"). Changes of state (loading, empty, error) are written once to a polite live region. \"View as table\"\napps/docs/pages/accessibility.md:12:| Keyboard | Up to two tab stops while the table is closed: the plot, and the toolbar when the chart has controls. A compact chart has none. Every point is reachable in order, the series can be switched, the ends are one key away, and Tab leaves the chart. The focus indicator is at least 2 px wide, in the focus colour, which meets 3:1 on the default backgrounds; it always shows while you use the keyboard in the plot, and it may also show after a click. |\napps/docs/pages/accessibility.md:14:| Colour-vision deficiency | Eight colours chosen to stay apart under simulated protanopia, deuteranopia and tritanopia, and never colour alone: lines and areas carry marker shapes, repeated in the legend and tooltip; bars by position and order, named in the legend and tooltip, and also by fill pattern past the eighth series and between neighbours in a compact chart, which has no legend; adjacent fills are separated by background. |\napps/docs/pages/accessibility.md:53:| So small that the strip would leave the plot under 80 px high | No readout. The datum is highlighted; its values are in the plot's keyboard reading and in the table. |\napps/docs/pages/accessibility.md:61:**It never takes the pointer.** The pointer passes through a tooltip to the plot, so the readout follows every move and a click lands on the plot. To reach the tooltip, rest the pointer on the datum for a quarter of a second: the datum is then held while the pointer travels towards its tooltip and while it is over it. Leaving that path picks again at once; a pointer that moves without resting is never held. A click always acts on what is under the pointer, whatever is held.\napps/docs/pages/accessibility.md:63:**A click or tap pins.** It selects the datum under the pointer (the `select` event) and pins its tooltip, or its strip. Pinned, the content takes the pointer and is exposed to assistive technology: each row is a button of at least 24 × 24 px that selects that series' value, and \"+N more\" becomes a button that shows the next rows. The rows are outside the tab order; from the keyboard, the plot's Up and Down reach the same series. The pin ends with Escape, a click elsewhere, a drag or pinch, or when its datum leaves the view. With `tooltip: false`, or in a plot with no readout, nothing is pinned; a click still selects. While Select range\napps/docs/pages/accessibility.md:74:- **A pause for streaming compact tiles.** A full chart fed by `append` has its own Pause control. A compact chart has no toolbar, so it has none: if your tiles stream, give the reader a control of your own that stops appending, and append what arrived meanwhile on resume (WCAG 2.2.2).\napps/docs/pages/theming.md:63:The dark scheme's focus colour is a near-white, so that the first dark series could be an ordinary dark-mode blue. Colour is never the only cue: lines and areas also carry marker shapes, bars are told apart by position and named by the legend and tooltip (with fill patterns past the eighth series, and in a compact chart), adjacent fills are separated by background, and the legend and tooltip repeat the cues.\n   \"totalGzipBytes\": 86219,\n   \"totalKiB\": 84.2,\n   \"targetKiB\": null,\n   \"targetBytes\": null,\n   \"over\": false\n  },\n  {\n   \"entry\": \"validate only (export { validate } from axylo)\",\n   \"minBytes\": 58597,\n   \"jsGzipBytes\": 21141,\n   \"cssFileGzipBytes\": 0,\n   \"totalGzipBytes\": 21141,\n   \"totalKiB\": 20.65,\n   \"targetKiB\": null,\n   \"targetBytes\": null,\n   \"over\": false\n  },\n  {\n   \"entry\": \"script tag (axylo.iife.min.js + axylo.css)\",\n   \"minBytes\": 336166,\n   \"jsGzipBytes\": 118341,\n   \"cssFileGzipBytes\": 3042,\n   \"totalGzipBytes\": 121383,\n   \"totalKiB\": 118.54,\n   \"targetKiB\": null,\n   \"targetBytes\": null,\n   \"over\": false\n  }\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
