{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n 'defer|[Pp]ostpone|[Aa]fter 1.0|[Ll]ater|[Oo]ut of scope|[Nn]on.goal|[Nn]ot in 1.0' docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md | cut -c1-820\"","aggregated_output":"123:| Two phases | Each call validates and normalizes synchronously: diagnostics are current when it returns, and a `strict` chart throws from that call. Drawing is deferred to the next animation frame, and calls made before that frame are drawn once. |\n128:| Teardown | `destroy()` cancels the pending frame, disconnects observers and listeners, and makes later calls no-ops. |\n149:### 1.7 React adapter (not in 1.0)\n151:Removed from the first release by the owner on 2026-11-18: 1.0 is plain TypeScript, and adapters for React, Vue, Svelte and others follow later. The adapter described below was built, reviewed twice and fixed; its last state is `packages/react` at commit dac5664. The section is kept as the design to return to.\n400:## 6. What waits until after 1.0\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat apps/docs/pages/compact.md && sed -n '47,61p' apps/docs/pages/accessibility.md && sed -n '1,48p' apps/docs/pages/theming.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Compact charts\n\nAfter this page you can put a sparkline in a KPI tile with `compact: true`, know what it shows and leaves out, and give it a text alternative.\n\n## When to use it\n\n`compact: true` on a line, area or bar chart draws the marks and nothing else: no axes, gridlines, legend, toolbar, table control, zoom, tooltip or reference lines. It is for a small tile where the page already shows the number and its label, and the chart only says which way it is going.\n\n[example: compact-tiles](../examples/compact-tiles.html)\n\nA compact chart does not give access to every value; its summary is the alternative. When the reader needs every value, use the full chart.\n\n## What a compact chart is\n\n| Behaviour | Compact |\n|---|---|\n| For assistive technology | An image (`role=\"img\"`) with an accessible name and the generated summary as its description: series, x range, minimum, maximum, last value, any values left out, and for stacks the positions where a part is missing (named up to three, counted beyond). |\n| Name | `labelledBy` with the tile heading's id (the heading is usually already there), or `title`. In a compact chart the title is not shown; it is the accessible name. Without either, the name is generated and [AX140](diagnostics.md#ax140) is raised. |\n| Keyboard and pointer | No tab stop and no pointer behaviour, so nothing is available to a mouse that is not available to a keyboard. A page of forty tiles adds no tab stops. |\n| Streaming | `append` works as on a full chart, but there is no Pause control, since there is no toolbar. If tiles stream, the page must give the reader its own way to pause them (WCAG 2.2.2): a control that stops appending, and appends what arrived meanwhile on resume. |\n| Size | Fills its container from 24 × 16 px up. With no height set, it is 32 px tall. |\n| Several series | Allowed, told apart by dash (lines) or fill pattern (bars) as well as colour, since there is no legend. Bars side by side always differ by pattern: the first series is plain, the next ones take diagonal lines, crosses and dots in turn, and colour and pattern together give 32 combinations before one repeats. Hint [AX131](diagnostics.md#ax131) suggests one series per tile. |\n| Fidelity | The rules of the full charts still hold: zero in the value axis for bar and area, gaps for missing values, diagnostics, simplified drawing of dense lines, sorting by x, no motion, and light or dark from the page. |\n\n## Options\n\n`compact` takes the options of its chart type ([line](line.md#options), [area](area.md#options), [bar](bar.md#options)). `zoom`, `legend`, `tooltip` and `table` have no effect on a compact chart, since it has no controls. Three things do not apply and are reported as such ([AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150)): `reference`, since a compact chart draws no references (so a reference far from the data cannot flatten the sparkline); a `tickFormat` on `x` or `y`, since it draws no axes; and a `chart.zoom()` call, which changes nothing and is not kept for later. Scatter and donut have no compact form: on them `compact` is a type error.\n\n## Edge case: a value left out\n\nWhen values were left out, the description says so and a small marker in the corner of the chart carries the same text as a tooltip.\n\n[example: compact-dirty](../examples/compact-dirty.html)\nPointing anywhere in the plot shows what is there, by the chart type's rule: on line and area charts the nearest x, on bars the band under the pointer, on scatter charts the nearest point within 24 px, on a donut the slice under the pointer. What is shown depends on the room the plot has:\n\n| Plot | What pointing shows |\n|---|---|\n| At least 240 × 140 px | A floating tooltip. |\n| Narrower than 240 px or shorter than 140 px | The same content, written as running text (\"x: series value; series value; +2 more\") in a strip of two lines reserved under the plot. The strip covers nothing, and nothing covers it. |\n| So small that the strip would leave the plot under 80 px high | No readout. The datum is highlighted; its values are in the plot's keyboard reading and in the table. |\n\nThe choice is made by layout, when the chart is laid out, never by the pointer.\n\n**Where the tooltip goes.** It is placed against the marks of the datum it describes, never at the pointer: above them first, then below, then to the right or the left (for horizontal bars: right, left, above, below); a donut's tooltip goes in its hole first, when it fits there. It stays inside the plot, the viewport and any container that clips the chart, and covers neither those marks, a control nor the focus ring; rows that do not fit are counted in \"+N more\". Only when not even one row fits anywhere may it partly cover the marks, never a control or the focus ring, and the table still has every value.\n\n**Long text.** A value that fits stays on one line with its unit; a longer one wraps inside the tooltip, which is never wider or taller than the visible part of the plot. However long the text, the tooltip shows its heading and at least one row, cut if they must be; it is left out only when every place for it would cover a control or the focus ring. In the strip, entries wrap within its two lines, and when not even one fits, the x and one row are cut to one line with an ellipsis. Its \"+N more\" is shown whole or the strip is not shown.\n\n**It never takes the pointer.** The pointer passes through a tooltip to the plot, so the readout follows every move and a click lands on the plot. To reach the tooltip, rest the pointer on the datum for a quarter of a second: the datum is then held while the pointer travels towards its tooltip and while it is over it. Leaving that path picks again at once; a pointer that moves without resting is never held. A click always acts on what is under the pointer, whatever is held.\n# Theming\n\nAfter this page you can change a chart's colours and type with CSS custom properties, give a series the same colour on every chart, and know what Axylo does in dark mode, in forced-colours mode and in print, and why its default palette looks the way it does.\n\nThere is no theme object and no palette option. A chart takes its font and text colour from the page, as any element does, and everything else from CSS custom properties that you can set on the chart, on any ancestor, or on `:root`.\n\n## The custom properties\n\nEach colour property falls back to a default from Axylo's stylesheet, with one value for light charts and one for dark. The defaults below are read from the packed package's stylesheet when this site is built.\n\n[table: theme tokens](../data/tokens.mjs)\n\nValues are any CSS colour, including `var()` references; a chart follows a variable when it changes, without a call. Colours of marks should keep at least 3:1 against the background behind the chart, and text 4.5:1. When the series colours fall below 3:1 against the background the chart actually sits on, Axylo raises [AX141](diagnostics.md#ax141); when that background cannot be resolved (a background image on the way up), it does not judge.\n\n[example: theme-tokens](../examples/theme-tokens.html)\n\n## One colour per series\n\nThe palette assigns colours by position: the first series gets the first colour. On a dashboard where charts show different subsets, \"Referral\" would change colour from chart to chart. `colors` pins a colour to a series key, so the same series looks the same everywhere:\n\n```ts\nconst colors = { Direct: 'var(--brand-direct)', Referral: 'var(--brand-referral)' };\nline('#a', { ...options, colors });\n```\n\nThe key is the series key: for long rows the `series` field's value (as above), for wide rows the field name, not its `label`, and for a donut the slice label. Series not named in `colors` take palette colours. A value the browser does not accept as a colour is replaced by the series' palette colour. A donut's generated \"Other\" slice always uses the muted colour.\n\n[example: theme-colors](../examples/theme-colors.html)\n\nWith wide rows, key the colours by field name:\n\n[example: wide-keys](../examples/wide-keys.html)\n\n## Dark mode\n\nEach chart decides on its own whether it is light or dark, from the page around it:\n\n1. If `--axylo-background` is set, from that colour's lightness.\n2. Otherwise from the chart element's computed `color-scheme`: `light` or `dark`; for `light dark`, the scheme the browser is using there.\n3. Otherwise from the inherited text colour: light text means a dark page.\n\nSo a page that sets `color-scheme`, a page whose theme switch only changes classes and colours, and a light-only page on a device set to dark mode (which stays light) all get the right defaults. The chart looks again when `prefers-color-scheme`, `forced-colors` or `prefers-contrast` change, and when the `class`, `style` or `data-theme` attribute of the chart or any of its ancestors changes, and repaints in the next frame. A theme switch that changes none of these (a stylesheet swapped in, say) is not observed.\n\n[example: theme-toggle](../examples/theme-toggle.html)\n\n## Forced colours, high contrast and print\n\nIn forced-colours mode (Windows High Contrast and similar) every paint becomes a system colour, the colours you set included, and they come back when the mode ends. Series are then told apart by their markers, dashes and patterns. Under `prefers-contrast: more`, and in forced colours, lines also differ by dash. No example on this site emulates forced colours; the library's own browser tests do.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat packages/axylo/src/index.ts && rg -n 'renderer|SVG' docs/decisions/0002* | cut -c1-420 && sed -n '1,33p' apps/docs/pages/diagnostics.md && rg '\"'^'\"\\\\| AX\\\\d+' docs/02-product-spec.md | cut -d'|' -f3 | sort | uniq -c\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat packages/axylo/src/index.ts && rg -n 'renderer|SVG' docs/decisions/0002* | cut -c1-420 && sed -n '1,33p' apps/docs/pages/diagnostics.md && rg '\"'^'\"\\\\| AX\\\\d+' docs/02-product-spec.md | cut -d'|' -f3 | sort | uniq -c\"","aggregated_output":"/**\n * Axylo's public entry points. Importing this module touches no DOM, so it loads in Node, where `validate`\n * runs; the chart functions need a document and are only called in a browser.\n */\nimport type {\n  AreaOptions, BarOptions, Chart, Diagnostic, DonutOptions, LineOptions, ScatterOptions, Target,\n} from './public.js';\nimport { staticDiagnostics } from './core/stages';\nimport { viewerTimeZone } from './core/environment';\nimport { createChart } from './core/chart';\nimport { AREA } from './kinds/area';\nimport { BAR } from './kinds/bar';\nimport { DONUT } from './kinds/donut';\nimport { LINE } from './kinds/line';\nimport { SCATTER } from './kinds/scatter';\n\nexport type * from './public.js';\n\nexport function line<Row>(target: Target, options: LineOptions<Row>): Chart<Row, LineOptions<Row>> {\n  return createChart(LINE, target, options) as unknown as Chart<Row, LineOptions<Row>>;\n}\n\nexport function area<Row>(target: Target, options: AreaOptions<Row>): Chart<Row, AreaOptions<Row>> {\n  return createChart(AREA, target, options) as unknown as Chart<Row, AreaOptions<Row>>;\n}\n\nexport function bar<Row>(target: Target, options: BarOptions<Row>): Chart<Row, BarOptions<Row>> {\n  return createChart(BAR, target, options) as unknown as Chart<Row, BarOptions<Row>>;\n}\n\nexport function scatter<Row>(target: Target, options: ScatterOptions<Row>): Chart<Row, ScatterOptions<Row>> {\n  return createChart(SCATTER, target, options) as unknown as Chart<Row, ScatterOptions<Row>>;\n}\n\nexport function donut<Row>(target: Target, options: DonutOptions<Row>): Chart<Row, DonutOptions<Row>> {\n  return createChart(DONUT, target, options) as unknown as Chart<Row, DonutOptions<Row>>;\n}\n\n/**\n * Runs the synchronous stages of a create call without a DOM (option resolution, normalization, the view's\n * domain and series checks, naming) and returns the same call-origin diagnostics a created chart reports\n * (plan 1.6). Each chart type takes its own options, so an option that does not apply does not compile.\n */\nexport function validate<Row>(type: 'line', options: LineOptions<Row>): Diagnostic[];\nexport function validate<Row>(type: 'area', options: AreaOptions<Row>): Diagnostic[];\nexport function validate<Row>(type: 'bar', options: BarOptions<Row>): Diagnostic[];\nexport function validate<Row>(type: 'scatter', options: ScatterOptions<Row>): Diagnostic[];\nexport function validate<Row>(type: 'donut', options: DonutOptions<Row>): Diagnostic[];\nexport function validate(type: 'line' | 'area' | 'bar' | 'scatter' | 'donut', options: unknown): Diagnostic[] {\n  return staticDiagnostics(type, options, viewerTimeZone());\n}\n1:# Decision 0002: SVG paths draw the marks by default (provisional)\n3:Status: **provisional working default**, set by the coordinator on 2026-11-18 from the M1 measurements and weakened the same day after an independent review of the benchmark harness (`reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/bench.review.md`, Codex gpt-6.2-sol at effort max). The owner asked for this to be settled by measurement. It is not settled: the measurements support using SVG while the line-chart slice is built and keepin\n10:- **Withdrawn: the resize CPU gap is raster work.** The number is CPU for the whole renderer process; no stage was traced.\n12:- **Withdrawn: an SVG theme change needs no run-time work.** Both renderers recorded six DOM mutations per toggle; \"no canvas drawing call\" is all that was observed.\n14:- **Narrowed: \"half the renderer memory.\"** The halving is of the increase above a data-only page at pixel ratio 2 (34.2 → 17.7 MiB). Absolute renderer memory was 74.7 → 56.2 MiB, and at pixel ratio 1 the increase was 23.1 → 18.4 MiB.\n16:- **Void: every 24-chart page row** (page at pixel ratio 1 and 2, resize of the 24-chart page, and the memory row). The harness fix pass found that these runs drew no marks: at 230 × 115 px each tile is below the 120 × 80 px plot minimum of spec L8, so every chart showed its text fallback. The SVG records carry `svgPaths: 0`; the canvas runs held empty canvases. The numbers compare two empty charts and say nothing\n17:- **Narrowed: verdicts.** 149 metric comparisons were judged without adjustment for choosing among them, on six pairs or fewer per workload, with noise bands from one session and two workload shapes. Two \"SVG lower\" rows allow a gain as small as 0.16 ms (10,000 points) and 6.3 ms (dashboard at pixel ratio 2).\n19:Evidence: [bench/results/m1-renderer-comparison.md](../../bench/results/m1-renderer-comparison.md). One session of 39 runs, production build, headless Chrome 157, machine under load from other work, A-versus-A noise bands for two workloads only.\n23:Canvas → SVG, medians of main-thread task time unless stated. \"Lower\" means the 95% interval cleared zero and the noise band where one existed.\n25:| Workload | Canvas | SVG | Verdict |\n27:| Streaming, per update | 2.26 ms | 1.45 ms | SVG lower |\n28:| Theme change, per toggle | 2.68 ms and 27 drawing calls | 2.01 ms and no drawing call | SVG lower |\n29:| Resize, one chart of 100,000 points | 915 ms | 472 ms | SVG lower on the main thread |\n30:| Same, CPU across all threads | 1,035 ms | 1,770 ms | **SVG higher** |\n32:| First chart, 10,000 points | 34.0 ms | 28.4 ms | SVG lower |\n37:| Memory, 24-chart page at pixel ratio 2 | 34.2 MiB renderer, 11.5 MiB GPU, 7.7 MiB backing stores | 17.7 MiB, 2.2 MiB, none | Void: no marks were drawn |\n41:## Why SVG for now\n43:1. On the line workloads measured, main-thread task time was lower with SVG or the two could not be told apart.\n44:2. Line series are simplified to a few vertices per pixel column before either renderer sees them, so for line charts the path size follows the plot width, not the data.\n45:3. SVG follows the page with less code: forced colors and print work through CSS, browser zoom stays sharp, and there is no backing store to size, cap or lose. This is a design argument, not a measurement.\n53:- Both renderers are far from uPlot in the configurations measured (about 80 ms against 12 ms at 100,000 points; uPlot draws no tooltip or accessibility layer). Where that time goes is not established.\n57:- The working default is SVG. The canvas renderer stays in the code behind the same internal switch, with the pixel-agreement tests between the two kept.\n58:- Before the choice is final: fix the harness defects the review found (renderer identity in the readback, noise bands on replay, failed runs excluded from speed verdicts, gap-aware admission, the frame window), declare the primary endpoints and thresholds in advance, then rerun on a quiet machine with scatter at 10,000, 50,000 and 200,000 points, ten series of 100,000 points, pan and zoom, and the byte cost of ke\n60:- The plan's sections 2.2 and 2.3 are read with SVG in the \"marks\" position: a theme change no longer needs a redraw, and device pixel ratio matters only to simplification.\n# Diagnostics\n\nAfter this page you can read an Axylo diagnostic, collect diagnostics in your own code, make a chart throw on them, and check a chart's data in Node or in a test before anything is drawn. Every code has its own page, listed at the end.\n\n## Reading one\n\nWhen Axylo cannot draw something as given, it prints one line to the console:\n\n[output: AX103](../data/diagnostics.mjs)\n\n| Part | Here |\n|---|---|\n| Code | `AX103`. Stable across releases; each has a page. |\n| Chart | `\"Conversion\"`: the chart's title, or its element id when it has none. |\n| What and where | The field, how many values, and the first rows (up to ten), numbered in your own data as you passed it. A diagnostic about the chart as a whole (no name, too many series, low contrast) has no field and no rows. |\n| Fix | What to change. |\n| Outcome | What the chart did instead: here, gaps. |\n\n## Levels and who is told\n\n| Level | Means | Console |\n|---|---|---|\n| Error | Something cannot be drawn as given: the chart, a series, or the donut. | Every build, once per code per page, with `console.warn` |\n| Warning | Something is not drawn as given, or an option is ignored. | Every build, once per code per page, with `console.warn` |\n| Hint | Advice for you. | Development builds only, with `console.info` |\n\nThe level says who is told; it does not by itself say what stays on screen. That depends on the code, and each code's page says it, with a checked minimal input. For example: an error about one series ([AX108](diagnostics.md#ax108)) leaves the other series drawn; a warning can put the chart in its error state when the ignored option was needed to draw at all (a `y` list on a scatter chart, [AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150)); and some hints come with a visible change: an inferred missing step is drawn as a gap ([AX105](diagnostics.md#ax105)), a plot too small to draw is replaced by its summary ([AX142](diagnostics.md#ax142)), hidden labels get a note ([AX143](diagnostics.md#ax143)).\n\nWhen values were left out or changed, the chart shows the reader a note in the figure (\"Values not shown: 3. See table.\", or \"Values shown differently from the data\"), in every build, and in the accessibility tree.\n\n## Collecting them in your code\n\nEach diagnostic is an object:\n      6  Error \n     10  Hint \n     13  Warning \n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
